const EVENTS = [
  item("mulhall-corbett-dc-1880",1880,"10 Jun 1880","Two future-family households appear on one DC census page","smith","Work","proposed","An original sheet names John Mulhall's Irish-born parents and shows a likely Corbett connection nearby.","John T. Mulhall, 25, was a brass molder living with parents John and Mary, both born in Ireland. John Sr. worked as a gardener; brothers Joseph and Frank were painters. On the same page, William Corbett, an Ireland-born laborer, had DC-born daughters Maggie, 20, who worked in a store, and Annie, 18. Their households were enumerated in adjacent dwellings 277 and 278.","The John T. match with Alice's later father is strong by name, birthplace and trade. Identifying nearby Maggie as his future wife and William as her father is probable, not proved by a marriage or parent-naming record. Ashley's link through James Smith also remains probable.","Washington, DC",["John Thomas Mulhall, probable Ashley ancestor","John Mulhall, Ireland-born","Mary Mulhall, Ireland-born","William Corbett, probable relative","Maggie Corbett, probable match","Annie Corbett, possible sister"],[["Saved original 1880 census",ROOT+"sources/records/mulhall-corbett-neighbor-households-census-1880.jpg"],["John T. household index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M6CZ-3JM?lang=en"],["Maggie household index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M6CZ-3NP?lang=en"]],"branches/smith-mulhall-corbett.md"),
  item("mulhall-dc-1900",1900,"9 Jun 1900","Alice Mulhall's family reports Ireland-born grandparents","smith","Home","proposed","A saved census shows a probable Ashley branch with two DC-born adults and four Ireland-born parents.","John and Margaret Mulhall lived with Eliza, Mary, John T. and five-year-old Alice in Washington. John worked as a brass molder and they rented. Both adults said they were born in DC and that each of their parents was born in Ireland. An 1894 birth-return index and Alice's 1937 Social Security index independently name her parents John T. Mulhall and Margaret Corbett.","The 1880 census names John and Mary Mulhall; William Corbett is a probable father for Margaret, but her parentage and all immigration dates remain unverified. Ashley's link to this branch remains probable because the 1957 James–Gladys license notice does not identify James's parents. FamilySearch's index reads Alice as six; the original has five.","Washington, DC",["Alice M. Mulhall Smith, probable Ashley ancestor","John Thomas Mulhall","Margaret Corbett Mulhall","Eliza Mulhall","Mary Mulhall","John T. Mulhall Jr."],[["Original 1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/mulhall-dc-household-census-1900.jpg"],["1894 birth-return index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:F75L-ZVW?lang=en"],["1937 Social Security index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6K48-HYTQ?lang=en"],["Period DC street view",ROOT+"sources/places/washington-dc-se-street-1900-1905.jpg"]],"branches/smith-mulhall-corbett.md"),
  item("mulhall-dc-1910",1910,"16 Apr 1910","John Mulhall becomes a water-department foreman","smith","Work","proposed","Two original censuses show a trade change in the probable Smith branch.","The Washington household again contains John, Margaret, Mary, John Jr. and Alice. The 1910 original lists John as a District water-department foreman, Mary as a dry-goods saleswoman and John Jr. as a printer. In 1900 John was a brass molder.","No record connects him to a particular reservoir or shows a rise in household wealth. The 1910 index's age 38 for John conflicts with the 1900 census age 47. This remains a conditional Ashley branch until the younger James's identity is proved.","Washington, DC",["John Thomas Mulhall","Margaret Corbett Mulhall","Alice M. Mulhall Smith, probable Ashley ancestor"],[["Original 1910 census",ROOT+"sources/records/mulhall-dc-household-census-1910.jpg"],["Original 1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/mulhall-dc-household-census-1900.jpg"]],"branches/smith-mulhall-corbett.md"),
  item("mulhall-smith-dc-1920",1920,"15–16 Jan 1920","Raymond Smith is named as the Mulhalls' son-in-law","smith","Home","proposed","A two-page original joins Alice's parents, husband and first daughter under one roof.","In a rented Washington home, John T. and Margaret Mulhall lived with Raymond J. Smith, expressly called son-in-law. The next page continues the same family with Alice M. Smith, called daughter, and Margaret B. Smith, 1 year 10 months, called granddaughter. Raymond worked as an optician at an optical shop.","The original directly proves this Mulhall–Smith relationship, but it does not prove their later son James was the 1957 groom Gladys Blevins married. The older adults' ages disagree with earlier censuses; no rent amount or wages appear.","Washington, DC",["John Thomas Mulhall","Margaret Corbett Mulhall","Raymond J. Smith, probable Ashley ancestor","Alice M. Mulhall Smith, probable Ashley ancestor","Margaret B. Smith"],[["1920 original, page 14B",ROOT+"sources/records/mulhall-smith-household-census-1920-page1.jpg"],["Continuation, page 15A",ROOT+"sources/records/mulhall-smith-household-census-1920-page2.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33S7-9R6Q-NS3?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AMNLL-LRN&action=view&cc=1488411&lang=en"]],"branches/smith-mulhall-corbett.md"),
  item("pietra-magro-birth",1879,"24 Jun 1879, indexed","Pietra Magro is born in Sutera","cordaro","Birth","recorded","An original Sicilian birth entry anchors the Magro side of Justin's family.","Act 62 in Sutera's 1879 birth register names the child Magro Petra in its margin. The indexed entry gives 24 June and parents Onofrio Magro and Carmela Di Prima. Both parents recur in the 1899 Cordaro–Magro marriage notices, and Carmela appears as Pietra's mother and contact in the 1909 passenger list.","The archival image is faint. The day and parent names are reported by the index and cross-record comparison, not a complete new transcription of both original pages. Sutera and Milocca, then a hamlet of Sutera, vary as birthplace labels.","Sutera, Caltanissetta, Sicily",["Pietra / Petra Magro","Onofrio Magro","Carmela Di Prima"],[["Original act, scan 47","https://antenati.cultura.gov.it/ark:/12657/an_ua18882638/0Myg1d7"],["Continuation, scan 48","https://antenati.cultura.gov.it/ark:/12657/an_ua18882638/57QZmvO"],["FamilySearch index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QV98-LDK9?lang=en"],["1899 banns",ROOT+"sources/records/cordaro-magro-banns-1899.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md#three-generation-record-path"),
  item("antonio-cordaro-crossing",1906,"April 1906, reported","Antonio Cordaro reports a Sicilian crossing","cordaro","Migration","recorded","His later citizenship papers put an ocean crossing in April 1906.","Antonio's 1927 petition reports leaving Palermo on 8 April and arriving in New York on 20 April. A same-name 23 April Weimar passenger list is a strong candidate, but its Naples departure and later arrival differ. In 1927 Antonio lived in West Wyoming and worked as a miner.","The exact ship, arrival day and passenger identity are unresolved. This event dates Antonio's report, not an independently proved manifest.","Milocca / Palermo, Sicily → New York → West Wyoming, Pennsylvania",["Antonio Cordaro"],[["Signed 1927 petition",ROOT+"sources/records/antonio-cordaro-petition-1927.jpg"],["1906 candidate manifest",ROOT+"sources/records/antonino-cordaro-weimar-manifest-candidate-1906.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md#antonios-crossing-and-citizenship-an-original-record-chain"),
  item("pietra-cordaro-arrival",1909,"3 Sep 1909","Pietra and two daughters reach New York","cordaro","Migration","recorded","A passenger list joins their Sicilian and Pennsylvania family contacts.","Pietra Magro, 30, traveled in steerage with daughters Calogera Cordaro, 9, and Maria Cordaro, 4, on the Calabria. The ship left Palermo on 19 August and arrived New York on 3 September. Pietra named her mother Carmela Di Prima in Sutera and husband Antonio Cordaro in Pittsburgh. Joseph's later draft card says he was born in West Wyoming on 26 July 1910.","Pittsburgh is the intended destination on the manifest, not proof of residence there. The index misreads Pietra's surname as Magio. Antonio's own 1906 ship remains unresolved.","Sutera → Palermo, Sicily → New York → Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania",["Pietra Magro Cordaro","Calogera Cordaro","Maria Cordaro","Carmela Di Prima","Antonio Cordaro","Joseph Cordaro"],[["Original manifest, front",ROOT+"sources/records/pietra-cordaro-calabria-manifest-1909-front.jpg"],["Original manifest, reverse",ROOT+"sources/records/pietra-cordaro-calabria-manifest-1909-back.jpg"],["Joseph's draft card",ROOT+"sources/records/joseph-anthony-cordaro-draft-card-1940.jpg"],["FamilySearch index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:JX1V-G2J?lang=en"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md#antonios-crossing-and-citizenship-an-original-record-chain"),
  item("joseph-cordaro-draft",1940,"16 Oct 1940","Joseph Cordaro works for Lehigh Valley Coal","cordaro","Work","recorded","His signed draft card names his employer and Dina as wife.","Joseph Anthony Cordaro reported a 26 July 1910 birth in West Wyoming, Pennsylvania. At the October 1940 registration he lived there with wife Dina and named Lehigh Valley Coal Co. as his employer.","A draft card records registration, not military service or a specific coal-mining job. It gives no income or wealth figure.","West Wyoming, Pennsylvania",["Joseph Anthony Cordaro","Dina Caucci Cordaro"],[["Original draft card",ROOT+"sources/records/joseph-anthony-cordaro-draft-card-1940.jpg"],["FamilySearch index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:Q2SF-6CPM?lang=en"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md#three-generation-record-path"),
  item("antonio-cordaro-petition",1927,"23 Nov 1927","Antonio names his five children in a citizenship petition","cordaro","Home","recorded","The signed page joins immigrant Antonio to Justin's Joseph Cordaro.","Antonio's Scranton petition names wife Petra and children Calogera, Maria, Joseph, Carmela and Julius. The elder girls were born in Italy; Joseph and the younger children were born in West Wyoming. Joseph's 1938 marriage application independently names Anthony/Antonio and Petra as his parents.","FamilySearch's index labels the petition date as 1922, but the signed original says 1927. It does not establish that Calogera is the Fannie named in a census.","West Wyoming and Scranton, Pennsylvania",["Antonio Cordaro","Petra Magro Cordaro","Joseph Cordaro","Calogera Cordaro","Maria Cordaro","Carmela Cordaro","Julius Cordaro"],[["Original petition",ROOT+"sources/records/antonio-cordaro-petition-1927.jpg"],["Joseph's marriage application",ROOT+"sources/records/cordaro-caucci-marriage-1938.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md#antonios-crossing-and-citizenship-an-original-record-chain"),
  item("antonio-cordaro-naturalized",1928,"20 Nov 1928","Antonio Cordaro becomes a U.S. citizen","cordaro","Migration","recorded","A federal index gives the naturalization date and petition number.","The Scranton naturalization index says Antonio was naturalized on 20 November 1928, certificate 2710319, and references his original petition no. 19076. It names the same two merchant witnesses.","The final oath and citizenship certificate have not been inspected; the date comes from an official index card.","Scranton, Pennsylvania",["Antonio Cordaro"],[["Federal index card",ROOT+"sources/records/antonio-cordaro-naturalization-index-1928.jpg"],["Signed petition",ROOT+"sources/records/antonio-cordaro-petition-1927.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md#antonios-crossing-and-citizenship-an-original-record-chain"),
  item("halifax-tobacco-1939",1939,"Nov 1939","A tobacco truck crosses South Boston's main street","context","History","context","A period photograph shows the later market economy of Halifax County.","Marion Post Wolcott photographed a truck carrying tobacco through South Boston, Halifax County. Earlier Samuel and Jane Weatherford lived in Halifax; probable Asa and Julia were recorded there in the mid-nineteenth century. The photo helps picture the county's later commercial landscape, not the family's own property.","The picture was taken decades after those earlier Weatherfords' records. Garnett's reported Danville childhood was in a different nearby city; no family member is identified in the photo.","South Boston, Halifax County, Virginia",["Samuel and Jane Weatherford, earlier Halifax household"],[["Saved LOC photograph",ROOT+"media/south-boston-tobacco-1939.jpg"],["Library of Congress catalog","https://www.loc.gov/item/2017802158/"]],"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md"),
  item("nora-cowan-1892",1892,"15 Aug 1892","Nora Cowan is recorded in a Virginia birth register","smith","Birth","recorded","An original entry anchors Ashley's Cowan and Mason line.","Washington County's 1892 register names Nora J. Cowan and her parents Jasper and Mary. The 1900 census places her with three younger children and grandfather Isaac G. Cowan in Jasper's farming household. Later certificates give Mary's maiden name Mason and her father Henry Mason.","Nora's 1937 death certificate instead reports 14 August 1893. The contemporary birth register is preferred for her birth date; the disagreement is preserved.","Washington County, Virginia",["Nora Jane Cowan Hines","Jasper Newton Cowan","Mary Ann Mason Cowan","Isaac G. Cowan"],[["Original birth register",ROOT+"sources/records/nora-cowan-birth-register-1892.jpg"],["1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/cowan-household-census-1900.jpg"],["FamilySearch film","https://www.familysearch.org/en/search/film/004023748?i=00530"]],"branches/cowan-mason.md"),
  item("hines-bristol-1930",1930,"1920–31","Mary E. and Elizabeth Hines meet at one Bristol address","smith","Work","recorded","Two directories and two censuses reveal Mary's childhood household.","The 1930 census calls 17-year-old Mary E. a candy-factory packer at 912 Cumberland. The 1930–31 city directory lists Elizabeth, a candy packer, there with John J. and Nora J. Hines. The 1920 census has Elizabeth, 7, with John and Nora; the 1929 directory names John and Nora at the same later address. Later death certificates identify Nora as Nora Jane Cowan.","The 1930 census calls John's wife Maud while directories and both death certificates say Nora. The cause of that name difference and John's inconsistent birth state remain open. The address and job make the Mary/Elizabeth identity strong, not absolute.","Bristol, Virginia",["Mary Elizabeth Hines Blevins","John J. Hines","Nora Jane Cowan Hines"],[["1920 census",ROOT+"sources/records/hines-bristol-census-1920.jpg"],["1930 census",ROOT+"sources/records/hines-bristol-census-1930.jpg"],["1929 directory",ROOT+"sources/records/hines-bristol-directory-1929.png"],["1930–31 directory",ROOT+"sources/records/hines-bristol-directory-1930-31.png"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("howard-blevins-1930-candidate",1930,"10 Apr 1930","A Bristol furniture worker may be Gladys's father","smith","Work","proposed","A 1930 job points toward the 1940 varnish sprayer.","The original Bristol census lists W. Howard Blevins, 21, as a boarder with Robert L. Earnest and a laborer in a furniture plant. In 1940 Gladys's confirmed father W. Howard worked as a furniture-factory varnish sprayer. Name, age, place and industry support a possible identity bridge before his 1931 marriage to Mary E. Hines.","The 1930 man reported Virginia birth; Gladys's father reported West Virginia birth in 1940. The Earnest household called him a boarder, so it does not prove the proposed Earnest maternal link or name his parents.","Bristol, Virginia",["W. Howard Blevins, candidate","Robert L. Earnest"],[["Original 1930 census",ROOT+"sources/records/william-howard-blevins-candidate-census-1930.jpg"],["Confirmed 1940 household",ROOT+"sources/records/blevins-household-census-1940.jpg"]],"branches/blevins-deep-lineage.md"),
  item("nora-hines-1937",1937,"21 Feb 1937","Nora Cowan Hines dies after a Bristol car strike","smith","Death","recorded","Her certificate identifies another generation and a painful family event.","Nora's original Virginia certificate names husband and informant John J. Hines, parents Jasper N. Cowan and Mary Ann Mason, and a Bristol home on Solar Street. It records that a car struck her on 16 February and that she died five days later, aged 43.","The certificate does not identify the driver or a legal outcome. Its reported 1893 birth year conflicts with Nora's original 1892 birth entry; the 1930 census calls John's wife Maud.","Bristol, Virginia",["Nora Jane Cowan Hines","John Jackson Hines","Jasper N. Cowan","Mary Ann Mason"],[["Original 1937 certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/nora-jane-cowan-hines-death-1937.jpg"],["1892 birth register",ROOT+"sources/records/nora-cowan-birth-register-1892.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-99GG-S98C-Z?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AQVR4-VQ3Z&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("john-hines-1941",1941,"11 May 1941","John Hines's certificate meets Mary's Johnson City household","smith","Death","recorded","A shared address links three generations at 902 Watauga Avenue.","The 1940 census placed W. Howard and Mary H. Blevins with daughter Gladys at 902 East Watauga Avenue. John's 1941 death certificate gives 902 Watauga Avenue as his residence and Mrs. W. H. Blevins as informant. It identifies him as a widowed Southern Railroad machinist whose wife was Nora Jane Cowan.","This strongly supports the Mary–John connection but does not date John's move or prove a shared dwelling unit. His reported father James conflicts with a likely 1900 Bristol census John J., son of William Hines.","Johnson City, Tennessee",["John Jackson Hines","Mary Elizabeth Hines Blevins","William Howard Blevins","Gladys Louise Blevins Smith"],[["Original 1941 certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/john-jackson-hines-death-1941.jpg"],["1940 Blevins census",ROOT+"sources/records/blevins-household-census-1940.jpg"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("mary-blevins-1961",1961,"3 Dec 1961","Mary Blevins's notice links Gladys's childhood and married names","smith","Death","recorded","An original newspaper notice connects young Gladys Blevins to Gladys Lou Smith.","The Washington Evening Star notice says Mary E. Blevins died on 3 December 1961 at her Washington residence. It names husband William H. and children Mary Jane Griffith, Gladys Lou Smith, William V. and Bobby J. Blevins. Their names match the 1940 Johnson City household; Mary lived in Washington by 1961. The notice says she was buried at Washington National Cemetery.","A newspaper notice is not a civil death certificate. It does not name Mary's parents or date any family member's move; the 1940 census calls the older son William J., while the notice calls him William V.","Washington, DC",["Mary Elizabeth Hines Blevins","William Howard Blevins","Gladys Louise Blevins Smith","Mary Jane Blevins Griffith","William V. Blevins","Bobby Jack Blevins"],[["Original notice crop",ROOT+"sources/records/mary-hines-blevins-death-notice-1961.jpg"],["Library of Congress page","https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1961-12-05/ed-1/?sp=20"],["1940 family census",ROOT+"sources/records/blevins-household-census-1940.jpg"]],"branches/smith.md#gladyss-siblings-and-the-tancy-clue"),
  item("howard-hines-army-1942",1942,"2 Jan–3 Dec 1942","Howard Winston Hines registers, then enters the Army","smith","Military","recorded","Mary Hines's likely brother has a signed card and separate enlistment entry.","A 1940 census places Howard W. Hines, a house painter, with wife Gladys in her father Allen Lillie's home near Bristol. On 2 January 1942 Howard signed a draft card giving Keokee as birthplace and Gladys as wife/contact. An Army index records his 3 December enlistment as a private in Abingdon and reports two years of high school. In 1950 Howard and Gladys lived in Bristol with two children.","The same-name, age, location and trade sequence makes the identity strong, but the later records do not name Howard's parents. The enlistment entry gives no unit, deployment, combat or discharge; the education figure is reported in an index, not a school transcript.","Bristol and Abingdon, Virginia",["Howard Winston Hines","Gladys C. Hines","Mary Elizabeth Hines Blevins"],[["1940 household",ROOT+"sources/records/howard-hines-lillie-household-census-1940.jpg"],["Signed draft card",ROOT+"sources/records/howard-winston-hines-draft-card-1942.jpg"],["Army enlistment index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KMJG-D9J?lang=en"],["1950 household",ROOT+"sources/records/howard-hines-household-census-1950.jpg"]],"branches/smith.md#gladyss-siblings-and-the-tancy-clue"),
  item("blevins-1940-work",1940,"2 Apr 1940 census; 1939 wages","Gladys's father sprays varnish at a furniture factory","smith","Work","recorded","A wage and rent snapshot from young Gladys's home.","In Johnson City, W. Howard Blevins reported varnish-sprayer work in furniture manufacturing, 52 weeks worked and $1,000 in 1939 wages. The family rented for $12 a month. His one-year-old daughter G. Louise was in the household.","This census gives one year of wages and one month of rent, not the family's savings, debts or total wealth. It records Howard's birth in West Virginia, conflicting with earlier Howard candidates.","Johnson City, Tennessee",["William Howard Blevins","Mary Elizabeth Hines Blevins","Gladys Louise Blevins Smith"],[["Saved 1940 census",ROOT+"sources/records/blevins-household-census-1940.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-89M1-Z21V?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AK4HN-TVW&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("smith-dc-1930",1930,"2 Apr 1930","A probable Smith family rents in Washington, DC","smith","Home","proposed","An original census gives a concrete work and rent snapshot before James's birth.","Raymond J. and Alice M. Smith lived with four children: Margaret, Mary, William and Alice. Raymond was an optician at an optical company. Their reported rent was $35.50 per month; James A. was born later, in 1933 according to a fitting Social Security index.","The household is probable Ashley ancestry after a 1957 James–Gladys license notice, but the notice does not name James's parents. Neither rent nor occupation establishes wealth or uninterrupted employment.","Washington, DC",["Raymond J. Smith, probable ancestor","Alice M. Mulhall Smith, probable ancestor","Margaret B. Smith","Mary E. Smith","William J. Smith","Alice M. Smith"],[["1930 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/smith-dc-household-census-1930.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33S7-9R4C-ZMH?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AXM2M-BZD&action=view&cc=1810731&lang=en"]],"branches/smith-dc-candidate.md"),
  item("smith-dc-1950",1950,"1940–50","James A. grows up in a probable Smith branch in DC","smith","Work","proposed","Two censuses trace James A. Smith and his optician father.","The 1940 and 1950 Washington census sheets place James A. Smith with Raymond J. and Alice M. Smith. In 1950 Raymond again reports optician work. A 1917 marriage index spells Alice's maiden surname Mulhall; a 1966 obituary index names a son James A. A 1957 license notice later pairs a James Allen Smith of matching age with Gladys Blevins.","The 1957 notice does not name the groom's parents, so the DC son-to-Ashley-grandfather link is highly likely rather than proved. The 1940 census estimates James's birth about 1934; a fitting Social Security entry says 1933.","Washington, DC",["James A. Smith, probable match","Raymond J. Smith, probable great-grandfather","Alice M. Mulhall Smith, probable great-grandmother"],[["1930 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/smith-dc-household-census-1930.jpg"],["1940 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/smith-dc-household-census-1940.jpg"],["1950 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/smith-dc-household-census-1950.jpg"],["1957 license notice",ROOT+"sources/records/james-smith-gladys-blevins-marriage-license-notice-1957.jpg"]],"branches/smith-dc-candidate.md"),
  item("smith-blevins-license-1957",1957,"6 Nov 1957","James Smith and Gladys Blevins appear on a marriage-license list","smith","Marriage","recorded","A contemporary newspaper joins Ashley's grandparents before the Fairfax chapter.","The Evening Star's original page C-9 lists James Allen Smith, 24, of Channing Street NW, and Gladys Louise Blevins, 19, of Mellon Street SE, among DC marriage licenses. James's age fits the 14 October 1933 Social Security record, which names Raymond and Alice as parents.","A license notice is not the marriage certificate. It does not name the groom's parents, so the bridge from the DC childhood household to this groom remains highly likely rather than proved.","Washington, DC",["James Allen Smith","Gladys Louise Blevins"],[["Original newspaper page",ROOT+"sources/records/james-smith-gladys-blevins-marriage-license-notice-1957.jpg"],["Library of Congress page 77","https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045462/1957-11-06/ed-1/?sp=77"]],"branches/smith-dc-candidate.md"),
  item("carolyn-smith-marriage-1985",1985,"16 Nov 1985","Carolyn's marriage return names James and Gladys","smith","Marriage","recorded","A fourth child has an original parent-naming document.","Carolyn Louise Smith's Fairfax return names James Allen Smith and Gladys Louise Smith as her parents and records her marriage to James Treme in Alexandria. It reports a Washington, DC, birth, showing the couple's children had ties to DC before the later Miller Drive household.","The return does not identify James's parents or prove when Carolyn's family moved to Fairfax. FamilySearch attaches its index to an inconsistent 1904–1969 same-name tree person; the original is the evidence.","Alexandria, Virginia",["Carolyn Louise Smith Treme","James Allen Smith","Gladys Louise Blevins Smith"],[["Original 1985 return",ROOT+"sources/records/carolyn-smith-james-treme-marriage-1985.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C9TY-JFGX?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AQV1S-GYT6&action=view&cc=2370234&lang=en"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("bernhard-mill",1684,"1684","Bernhard Disqué offers to rebuild a mill","disque","Work","recorded","A Palatinate mill story begins in Siebeldingen.","A specialist mill history quotes Bernhard Disqué's offer to rebuild a ruined mill. It shows enterprise and access to a working site, though the surviving account gives no defensible income or net-worth figure.","The later chain from this family to Justin's Magdalena Disque remains a research question.","Siebeldingen, Palatinate",["Bernhard Disqué"],[["Mill-family history","https://www.eberhard-ref.net/pf%C3%A4lzisches-m%C3%BChlenlexikon/pf%C3%A4lzische-m%C3%BCllerfamilien/litera-d/"]],"branches/disque-schaaf.md"),
  item("sebastian-birth",1696,"1696, chart date","Sebastian Johann Disqué is born","disque","Birth","proposed","The family chart places Sebastian in Siebeldingen.","The inherited chart gives 12 November 1696. A specialist mill history documents Sebastian's later work and marriage, but this exact birth date still needs a church entry.","Treat the date as a search target, not a verified vital record.","Siebeldingen, Palatinate",["Sebastian Johann Disqué"],[["Chart transcription",ROOT+"research/chart-transcription.md"]],"branches/disque-schaaf.md"),
  item("disque-marriage",1718,"1718","Sebastian Disqué marries Anna Barbara Schaaf","disque","Marriage","recorded","A mill-working Palatinate family takes shape.","The specialist mill history reports their marriage and describes leasehold mills associated with Sebastian and different sons. These were productive assets, but there is no proof every descendant inherited a share.","The lineage from them to the later Pennsylvania Magdalena remains under review.","Palatinate, Germany",["Sebastian Disqué","Anna Barbara Schaaf"],[["Mill-family history","https://www.eberhard-ref.net/pf%C3%A4lzisches-m%C3%BChlenlexikon/pf%C3%A4lzische-m%C3%BCllerfamilien/litera-d/"]],"branches/disque-schaaf.md"),
  item("goochland-grants",1737,"15 Aug 1737","James Blevin receives two Goochland grants","blevins","Home","recorded","Patents describe 400 and 295 acres on Little Muddy Creek.","The Library of Virginia indexes two grants to James Blevin. Later deeds confirm transactions involving both tracts and call the seller James Blevin/Bleavin.","This James is not proved to be the later Smith River hunter, Revolutionary pensioner, or Ashley's ancestor.","Goochland County, Virginia",["James Blevin, Goochland landholder"],[["400-acre patent","https://lva.primo.exlibrisgroup.com/discovery/fulldisplay?docid=alma990007249560205756&context=L&vid=01LVA_INST:01LVA&lang=en"],["295-acre patent","https://lva.primo.exlibrisgroup.com/discovery/fulldisplay?docid=alma990007249570205756&context=L&vid=01LVA_INST:01LVA&lang=en"]],"branches/blevins-colonial-james.md"),
  item("virginia-1740-view",1740,"1740–70 image; plotted at 1740","A period view of colonial Virginia","context","History","context","A Williamsburg etching offers a contemporary image for the Goochland deed era.","The Library of Congress dates this etching broadly to 1740–70. It depicts public buildings in Williamsburg, Virginia's colonial capital. In the 1740s, tobacco and enslaved labor shaped the colony's economy, while settlement expanded into the Piedmont.","The picture is not a photograph or an image of Goochland, James Blevin's home or Ashley's confirmed ancestors. Its maker and exact year are uncertain; James's place in Ashley's direct line remains unproved.","Williamsburg, Virginia; separate from Goochland",["James Blevin, period context only"],[["Period etching, Library of Congress","https://www.loc.gov/item/2012649731/"],["Colonial views and context",ROOT+"context/virginia-colonial-views.md"]],"context/virginia-colonial-views.md"),
  item("goochland-deeds",1743,"1743–1745","James sells the Little Muddy Creek tracts","blevins","Home","recorded","Three deeds trace two sales and a correction.","Original Goochland deeds show a 295-acre sale in 1743, a 400-acre sale in 1744, and a corrective deed in 1745. The last calls the seller James Bleavin Senior of Brunswick County.","‘Senior’ distinguishes a namesake but by itself does not prove a father–son relationship.","Goochland and Brunswick counties, Virginia",["James Bleavin Senior"],[["Original deed links and reading",ROOT+"branches/blevins-colonial-james.md#the-goochland-landholder-in-his-own-records"]],"branches/blevins-colonial-james.md"),
  item("alexandria-plan-1749",1749,"1749","Alexandria appears as a planned Potomac town","context","History","context","The original plan marks streets and numbered lots beside the river.","The manuscript plan shows Alexandria, then called Belhaven, at its founding. It is a useful comparison with inland Goochland and later Fairfax County growth.","No identified ancestor is tied to a lot on this plan; Ashley's known Alexandria-area homes were in later Fairfax County.","Alexandria, Virginia",["No identified family member on this plan"],[["Library of Congress plan","https://www.loc.gov/item/98687108/"]],"context/virginia-colonial-views.md"),
  item("virginia-map-1755",1755,"1755","A map pictures Virginia after the Goochland deeds","context","History","context","The Fry–Jefferson map shows rivers, roads, counties and mountains.","This regional map was printed about a decade after James Blevin's Goochland transactions and helps locate the different parts of colonial Virginia.","It is not a 1740 snapshot, a survey of James's tract, or evidence that the Goochland James was Ashley's ancestor.","Virginia and neighboring colonies",["James Blevin, contextual lead only"],[["Library of Congress map","https://www.loc.gov/item/74693089/"]],"context/virginia-colonial-views.md"),
  item("pensioner-birth",1762,"c. 1761–1762","A different James Blevins recalls his birth","blevins","Birth","proposed","The future pensioner said he was born somewhere in New England.","In 1832 James recalled a lost family-Bible date of 25 December 1762, but his stated age implies 1761. He said his family moved to Virginia while he was an infant.","His father is unnamed. He cannot be the adult Goochland grantee of 1737; his connection to Ashley is unknown.","New England, exact town unknown",["James Blevins, Revolutionary pensioner"],[["Pension S32121 transcription","https://revwarapps.org/s32121.pdf"]],"branches/blevins-revolutionary-james.md"),
  item("peach-bottom",1772,"1772–1801","A James Blevins occupies Peach Bottom land","blevins","Home","proposed","A later lawsuit abstract links a James and son John.","An abstract of an 1805 land case says James held a Peach Bottom tract from 1772 until his death in 1801 and identifies John Blevins as a son. The original case file still needs reading.","Do not merge this James with either the Goochland landholder or the younger pensioner by name alone.","Peach Bottom, Virginia",["James Blevins, Peach Bottom","John Blevins"],[["Lawsuit abstract","https://yanceyfamilygenealogy.org/KenShirleyGenealogyBooks/blevins.pdf#page=168"]],"branches/blevins-colonial-james.md"),
  item("kings-mountain",1780,"7 Oct 1780","King’s Mountain changes the southern war","blevins","Military","proposed","James later claimed he fought with William Campbell’s Virginians.","Patriot militia surrounded Ferguson's Loyalists on a wooded South Carolina ridge. The roughly hour-long battle ended with Ferguson killed and his force defeated, disrupting Cornwallis's advance. James's 1832 pension testimony says he fought there; fellow volunteer John Stone confirmed James departed toward the battle but did not see him fight.","James's battlefield presence is a pension claim with partial corroboration. His descent link to Ashley remains unknown.","King’s Mountain, South Carolina",["James Blevins, pensioner","William Campbell","Patrick Ferguson"],[["James's pension testimony","https://revwarapps.org/s32121.pdf"],["National Park Service battle history","https://www.nps.gov/blri/learn/historyculture/overmountain-men.htm"]],"branches/blevins-revolutionary-james.md#what-happened-at-kings-mountain"),
  item("james-pension",1832,"12 Nov 1832","James gives his own migration and war account","blevins","Research","recorded","A sworn pension declaration preserves childhood moves and service claims.","In Lawrence County, Indiana, James described a New England birth, childhood in Virginia, southern campaigning, a later move near Knoxville and settlement in Indiana around 1821. A pension notation awards $20 annually for six months of Virginia militia service.","The pension file is evidence that he made these statements; its original images and his parentage remain to be checked.","Lawrence County, Indiana",["James Blevins, pensioner"],[["Pension S32121 transcription","https://revwarapps.org/s32121.pdf"]],"branches/blevins-revolutionary-james.md"),
  item("matthew-birth",1840,"29 Aug 1840, indexed baptism","Matthäus Kramer appears in a Baden birth register","kramer","Birth","proposed","An original German page names his parents, but his Pennsylvania identity is unproved.","The Unadingen register, entry 29, names Matthäus Kramer with Bernhard Kramer and Maria Huber. The inherited chart gives Matthew the same 1840 date but names his mother Mary Horner. The chart date may derive from this record; the 1870/1880 U.S. censuses give Bavaria and Württemberg for the possible same man.","Do not assign Unadingen or these parents to Ferdinand's line until a Pennsylvania-to-Germany identity bridge is found.","Unadingen, Baden — candidate only",["Matthäus Kramer, German-record candidate","Bernhard Kramer","Maria Huber"],[["Saved original German page",ROOT+"sources/records/unadingen-matthaeus-kramer-register-1840-candidate.jpg"],["Staatsarchiv Freiburg permalink","https://www.landesarchiv-bw.de/plink/?f=5-477660-85"],["Evidence comparison",ROOT+"branches/kramer-baden-candidate.md"]],"branches/kramer-baden-candidate.md"),
  item("knittelsheim-estate-1847",1847,"29 Sep 1847, advertised","An indivisible Disqué mill estate faces auction","disque","Work","proposed","A notice lists grain, oil and gypsum machinery, plus a hemp machine.","After miller Johann Sigmund Disqué died, a Knittelsheim auction notice described the mill, residence and farm buildings as an indivisible estate. A historian reports that the advertised auction apparently did not complete, leaving heirs with interests afterward.","The original notice, deed, final outcome and price have not been inspected. This miller is not yet linked through records to Justin's Pennsylvania Magdalena Disque.","Knittelsheim, Palatinate",["Johann Sigmund Disqué","Amalia Margaretha Disqué","Barbara Mathilde Disqué"],[["Mill history quoting auction notice","https://www.eberhard-ref.net/pf%C3%A4lzisches-m%C3%BChlenlexikon/pf%C3%A4lzische-m%C3%BChlen-u-m%C3%BChlorte/kleinottweiler-kusel/"],["Asset ledger",ROOT+"research/assets-by-generation.md"]],"branches/disque-schaaf.md"),
  item("jackson-weatherford-1844",1844,"9–10 Oct 1844","An Indiana Jackson marries Harriet McCampbell","weatherford","Research","recorded","A same-name record makes Samuel's proposed parentage harder to assume.","The original Parke County register records a license on 9 October and marriage on 10 October. A matching original 1850 Parke census lists Jackson with Harriet and daughter Eliza; he was 36 and Kentucky-born. A compiled tree assigns this 1844 marriage to a Jackson it dates born in 1783.","The marriage itself is documented, but this Indiana Jackson is not linked to Samuel Weatherford in Virginia. The census age conflicts with the compiled pedigree; it does not disprove the existence of an older Jackson.","Parke County, Indiana",["Jackson Weatherford, Indiana candidate","Harriet McCampbell"],[["Original marriage register",ROOT+"sources/records/jackson-harriet-weatherford-marriage-1844-indiana.jpg"],["Original 1850 census",ROOT+"sources/records/jackson-harriet-wetherford-census-1850-indiana.jpg"]],"branches/weatherford-jackson-candidate.md"),
  item("samuel-weatherford-1850",1850,"25 Oct 1850, enumerated","Samuel and Jane's Virginia-born household","weatherford","Home","recorded","Samuel is listed as a planter with $375 in real estate.","The original census places Samuel, Jane and 17-year-old Thomas together in Banister district and calls all three Virginia-born. It lists six younger household members; their school-attendance cells are blank for the census year. A 1855 marriage of Thomas E and Julia Oakes names Samuel and Jane as his parents; later records call George's father Asa T.","The census does not label the younger people's relationships or establish lifelong schooling, land title or slaveholding. The Thomas E / Asa T identification is probable but unresolved; Samuel and Jane's parents and an immigrant are unknown.","Halifax County, Virginia",["Samuel Wetherford","Jane Wetherford","Thomas / Asa Thomas Weatherford, proposed"],[["Saved 1850 census",ROOT+"sources/records/samuel-jane-weatherford-census-1850.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HT-DZ4Q-TDZ?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AM8D3-227&action=view&cc=1401638&lang=en"],["1855 marriage","https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:68TT-61CV?lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md#what-the-household-sheets-reveal"),
  item("samuel-weatherford-1870",1870,"19 Oct 1870, enumerated","Samuel's later Halifax household","weatherford","Work","recorded","A farmer with $786 real estate and $300 personal estate reported.","In Mount Carmel Township, the original census lists Samuel, Jane, Mary, Frances, Sarah and Caroline in one household. Samuel's 1860 estate fields were blank, so the three censuses do not establish an unbroken property trend across the Civil War.","These are nominal census entries, not net worth or land deeds. The 1870 schedule does not label the younger people's relationships.","Halifax County, Virginia",["Samuel Weatherford","Jane Weatherford","Mary, Frances, Sarah and Caroline Weatherford"],[["Saved 1870 census",ROOT+"sources/records/samuel-jane-weatherford-census-1870.jpg"],["Saved 1860 census",ROOT+"sources/records/samuel-jane-weatherford-census-1860.jpg"],["Resources visual",ROOT+"maps/samuel-weatherford-census-resources.svg"]],"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md#what-the-household-sheets-reveal"),
  item("samuel-weatherford-1880",1880,"10 Jun 1880","Samuel, Jane and Caroline in Black Walnut district","weatherford","Home","recorded","The original census pairs a late-life farm household with a daughter listed at school.","The enumerator listed Samuel, 70, as farmer, Jane, 60, as keeping house, and Caroline R., 22, as a daughter with occupation \"at school.\" All three reported Virginia birth and Virginia-born but unnamed parents. Samuel's health entry appears to say \"Varicose Veins.\"","The school-attendance column is blank even though Caroline's occupation says at school. The enumerator marked cannot-read and cannot-write for all three; these are one census's reports, not lifelong education histories. Jane's age conflicts with 1850. No property values were asked in 1880.","Black Walnut district, Halifax County, Virginia",["Samuel Wetherford","Jane Wetherford","Caroline R. Wetherford"],[["Saved original 1880 census",ROOT+"sources/records/samuel-jane-weatherford-census-1880.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33SQ-GYBJ-9CZ4?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AMC54-8NM&action=view&cc=1417683&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md#what-the-household-sheets-reveal"),
  item("weatherford-oakes-1855",1855,"19 Dec 1855, indexed","Thomas E. Weatherford and Julia Oakes marry","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","The full Halifax County register places their entry among their neighbors' marriages.","The indexed marriage names Thomas E. Weatherford, Julia Oakes, and Thomas's parents Samuel and Jane. The original two-page spread is saved so the entry can be checked against its column headings and neighboring rows.","Later records call George Weatherford's father Asa T.; Thomas E. and Asa T. may be the same man, but that identification is not settled.","Halifax County, Virginia",["Thomas E. Weatherford","Julia Oakes","Samuel Weatherford","Jane Weatherford"],[["Saved register spread",ROOT+"sources/records/halifax-weatherford-oakes-marriage-register-1855.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-L9XF-29M3-F?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3A68TT-61CV&action=view&lang=en&groupId=M98Y-PF6"]],"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md"),
  item("weatherford-household-1860",1860,"1860","A likely Asa and Julia household in Halifax","weatherford","Work","probable","William E bridges the 1857 birth record to later census households.","The original 1860 sheet groups indexed Acy T and July A Weatherford with Wm E, age four, and Charles T, age two. The man is recorded as an overseer with $300 personal estate and no real-estate value entered.","The names may be handwriting variants of Asa and Julia, but that reading needs checking. The census does not label relationships, employer, wages or land ownership; Charles T and 1870 Thomas are not yet confirmed as one person.","Halifax County, Virginia",["Acy T / Asa T Weatherford, proposed","July A / Julia A Weatherford, proposed","William E. Weatherford"],[["Original census sheet","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33SQ-GBS6-94FN?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AM41H-SQF&action=view&cc=1473181&lang=en"],["Evidence guide",ROOT+"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md"]],"branches/weatherford-early-virginia.md"),
  item("marshall-service",1862,"1862–1863","Marshall Miller serves with Pennsylvania militia","miller","Military","recorded","An 1890 veterans schedule names his unit.","The original veterans schedule lists Marshall Miller with Company K, 171st Pennsylvania Infantry and mentions fever and a regimental hospital. The household match to Gad Marshall Miller is strong.","The schedule does not place him in a named battle; a service or pension file is the next check.","Columbia County, Pennsylvania",["Gad Marshall Miller"],[["Original veterans schedule","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:939V-R9SW-RK?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AK89T-N4L&action=view&cc=1877095&lang=en"]],"context/civil-war-family-lines.md"),
  item("marshall-farm-1870",1870,"1870–1880","Marshall and Elizabeth raise Amos on a Sugarloaf farm","miller","Home","recorded","Original censuses connect the Union veteran's likely household to young Amos.","In 1870 Marshall was listed as a farmer with $600 real estate and Coles Creek post office. Elizabeth, Clarissa, Sarah, Amos and infant Nancy shared the home. The 1880 page explicitly calls 12-year-old Amos Marshall's son and adds Adaline, Miles, Jesse, Minnie and Murray.","The 1870 schedule does not label relationships; the veteran-to-farmer identification remains strong but inferred. $600 is one nominal census value, not net worth; 1880 gives no property value. Nancy and Adaline are likely one person, not proven by these pages alone.","Sugarloaf Township, Columbia County, Pennsylvania",["Marshall Miller","Elizabeth Miller","Amos Miller"],[["1870 original",ROOT+"sources/records/marshall-elizabeth-miller-census-1870.jpg"],["1880 original",ROOT+"sources/records/marshall-elizabeth-miller-census-1880.jpg"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#marshall-and-elizabeths-farm-household-187080"),
  item("disque-migration",1864,"1864","Magdalena Fröhlich’s documented family travels to America","disque","Migration","recorded","A widow and four named children left during a Palatinate migration peak.","An institutional card reports Magdalena Fröhlich's 1864 move with four children after her husband's 1853 death. Regional historians describe an 1864–1873 departure peak linked to divided farmland, crowded trades and earlier economic crises. These conditions may help explain the setting, but her own motive is not recorded.","The chart's Magdalena Disque, later said to marry Matthew Kramer, is absent from that child list. Her participation is unproved; the indexed route and original passenger list need comparison.","Palatinate → United States",["Magdalena Fröhlich Disqué"],[["Palatinate migration card","https://migration.pfalzgeschichte.de/person/98001"],["Regional historical exhibition","https://www.auswanderung-rlp.de/ziele-der-auswanderung/auswanderung-nach-nordamerika/ausstellung-zur-auswanderung-2010.html"]],"branches/disque-schaaf.md"),
  item("matthew-marriage",1866,"26 Aug 1866, chart date","Matthew Kramer and Magdalena Disque reportedly marry","kramer","Marriage","proposed","The family chart places this marriage in Wilkes-Barre.","The date and couple appear in the inherited chart. A church or civil record would establish the marriage and help separate Matthew, Matthias, and the documented 1864 migrant household.","Do not assume Magdalena was one of the four children on the 1864 migration card.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Matthew Kramer","Magdalena Disque"],[["Chart transcription",ROOT+"research/chart-transcription.md"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("kramer-arrival-report",1865,"1865, reported in 1900","Matthew and Lena Kramer report immigration","kramer","Migration","recorded","Both Germany-born parents gave 1865 as their U.S. arrival year.","The original 1900 Wilkes-Barre census places Matthew and Lena with their son Ferdinand and reports Germany as both parents' birthplace and 1865 as each immigration year. The 1870 and 1880 Martin/Lena households are strong earlier matches.","This is a later household report, not a manifest. The German town, voyage, motive and Martin/Matthew identity remain unproved.","Germany → Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Matthew Kramer","Lena Kramer","Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)"],[["Original 1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/kramer-matthew-lena-census-1900.jpg"],["Three-record analysis",ROOT+"branches/kramer-martin-migration.md"]],"branches/kramer-martin-migration.md"),
  item("martin-census-1870",1870,"1 Jun 1870","Martin Cramer reports property and masonry work","kramer","Work","probable","A Wilkes-Barre household reports $3,000 in real estate and $700 in personal estate.","The original census names Martin Cramer, stone mason, Lena and two young children. Both adults are listed as Bavaria-born. The figures are census asset values in 1870 dollars.","Martin's identity with Ferdinand's father Matthew is strong but not fully proved. This does not reveal debt, net worth, later sale or inheritance.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Martin Cramer","Lena Cramer"],[["Original 1870 census",ROOT+"sources/records/kramer-martin-lena-census-1870.jpg"],["Evidence analysis",ROOT+"branches/kramer-martin-migration.md"]],"branches/kramer-martin-migration.md"),
  item("martin-census-1880",1880,"9 Jun 1880","Martin Cramer works in a rope factory","kramer","Work","probable","The original census connects the likely earlier household to rope work.","Martin Cramer, wife Lena and five children are enumerated in Wilkes-Barre. Martin's birthplace is written Württemberg, differing from the 1870 Bavaria entry. Daughters Lena and Katie are marked at school.","The name and birthplace conflicts require another contemporary bridge to the 1900 Matthew household; no wage or property amount is reported.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Martin Cramer","Lena Cramer"],[["Original 1880 census",ROOT+"sources/records/kramer-martin-lena-census-1880.jpg"],["Evidence analysis",ROOT+"branches/kramer-martin-migration.md"]],"branches/kramer-martin-migration.md"),
  item("cordaro-birth",1878,"13 Jan 1878, passport reading","An Italian passport points to Sutera","cordaro","Birth","proposed","The bearer appears to be Onofrio Cordaro, a farm worker.","A family-held passport names Sutera, Caltanissetta and appears to give this birth date, a father Giuseppe and mother Maria. The photographed pages do not show when or whether he crossed the Atlantic.","A same-name child born in 1898 died in 1899 and is not this adult bearer. The 1878 birth, U.S. voyage and connection to Patricia Cordaro Kramer remain unproved.","Sutera, Sicily",["Onofrio Cordaro, provisional reading"],[["Family passport photograph",ROOT+"sources/family/italian-passport-details.jpg"],["1899 death index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:Q2HC-JQBM?lang=en"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("antonio-cordaro-birth",1878,"13 Jan 1878","Antonio Cordaro is born in Milocca","cordaro","Birth","recorded","His birth act names an agricultural worker father and a seamstress mother.","Original act no. 6 names Antonio Cordaro, father Giuseppe Cordaro, and mother Maria Tona. Milocca was then a hamlet of Sutera and is now Milena. The parents' occupations offer a glimpse of household work, not a measure of wealth.","The 1899 banns give the groom's mother a different apparent surname, Gorra/Carra. This 1878 child is only a possible match to that groom. The passport holder remains separate.","Milocca (now Milena), Sicily",["Antonio Cordaro","Giuseppe Cordaro","Maria Tona"],[["Original birth register, page 7","https://antenati.cultura.gov.it/ark:/12657/an_ua18544377/"],["Milena municipal history","https://www.comune.milena.cl.it/vivere-il-comune/territorio/la-storia/"],["Sicily map",ROOT+"maps/cordaro-sutera-places.svg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("cordaro-italy-marriage",1899,"19 Jun 1899","Antonio Cordaro and Pietra Magro request marriage notices","cordaro","Marriage","recorded","Their original banns name both sets of parents and describe their work.","Antonio, 21, a farm worker, and Pietra, 20, a homemaker, both lived in Milocca. The original names his parents Giuseppe and Maria Gorra/Carra (uncertain reading), and hers Onofrio Magro and Carmela Di Prima. A 1938 Pennsylvania application names Anthony and Petra as Joseph Cordaro's parents.","The banns establish intent, not a completed marriage. An index reports a 1 August ceremony and calls Antonio's mother Maria Tona; the original marriage act is needed to resolve the conflict.","Milocca, Caltanissetta, Sicily",["Antonio Cordaro","Pietra Magro","Giuseppe Cordaro","Maria Gorra / Carra","Onofrio Magro","Carmela Di Prima"],[["Original 1899 banns",ROOT+"sources/records/cordaro-magro-banns-1899.jpg"],["Indexed marriage","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6NCZ-7RPP?lang=en"],["1938 application",ROOT+"sources/records/cordaro-caucci-marriage-1938.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("ada-corderman-1900",1900,"4 Jun 1900 census","Ada Corderman lives with Wilson grandparents","miller","Home","recorded","Ada A., her siblings and widowed mother live with John and Sarah Wilson.","The original census places Ada A. Corderman, siblings Ella and Emery, and widowed mother Rosa E. in John and Sarah Wilson's Davidson Township household. A later census places a same-age Elmeda with William Reese nearby.","The 1900 page gives Ada an October 1890 birth, conflicting with Paulene's 1888 and a memorial's 1891. Her match to Elmeda Reese is strong but circumstantial.","Davidson Township, Sullivan County, Pennsylvania",["Ada A. Corderman","Rosa E. Corderman","John Wilson","Sarah Wilson"],[["Saved original census",ROOT+"sources/records/ada-corderman-wilson-household-1900.jpg"],["MyHeritage record","https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-10131-49221936/ada-a-corderman-in-1900-united-states-federal-census"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#ada-and-william-19001918"),
  item("reese-household-1910",1910,"10 May 1910 census","William and Elmeda Reese raise Ruth","miller","Home","recorded","The original census places the young Reese family in Davidson Township.","William Reese, 26, wife Elmeda, 20, and daughter Ruth, 1, share a Davidson Township household. The name, age and place suggest Elmeda was Ada A. Corderman from the 1900 Wilson household.","The 1910 schedule does not give Elmeda's maiden surname; the identity match still needs a marriage or vital record.","Davidson Township, Sullivan County, Pennsylvania",["William Reese","Elmeda Reese","Ruth Reese"],[["Saved original census",ROOT+"sources/records/elmeda-william-reese-household-1910.jpg"],["FamilySearch indexed record","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MG7J-QL3?lang=en"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#ada-and-william-19001918"),
  item("william-reese-draft-1918",1918,"12 Sep 1918","William Reese names Meda as nearest relative","miller","Work","recorded","His signed card records woods labor near Eagles Mere and a Muncy Valley home.","William Harrison Reese reported a 12 March 1884 birth, rural route 2 Muncy Valley residence, woods labor for Joseph Nolan near Eagles Mere, Meda Reese as nearest relative, and blindness in his right eye.","A draft card is not proof of service. FamilySearch's index appears to mistake his March birth day for the September registration date.","Muncy Valley and Eagles Mere, Pennsylvania",["William Harrison Reese","Meda Reese"],[["Saved original draft card",ROOT+"sources/records/william-harrison-reese-draft-card-1918.jpg"],["FamilySearch record","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:4D7K-94N2?lang=en"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#ada-and-william-19001918"),
  item("cordaro-arrival-window",1910,"By 1910, inferred","The Cordaro family settles in Pennsylvania","cordaro","Migration","recorded","Italy-born parents and a Pennsylvania-born son appear together in 1920.","The census records Anthony/Antonio and Petria/Pietra born in Italy, daughters Fannie and Mary born there, and son Joseph, about 10, born in Pennsylvania. Anthony reported arrival in 1895 and Petria in 1909. Pietra's 1909 arrival is now independently verified by the Calabria manifest.","Antonio's census arrival year conflicts with his later 1906 statement; his ship remains unidentified. The link between census Fannie and the Calogera named in the manifest and petition is not established.","Italy → Pennsylvania",["Antonio Cordaro","Pietra Magro","Joseph Cordaro"],[["1920 census","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M611-V9M?lang=en"],["1909 manifest",ROOT+"sources/records/pietra-cordaro-calabria-manifest-1909-front.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("ferdinand-older-birth",1882,"c. 1882","Ferdinand Kramer, the older foreman, is born","kramer","Birth","recorded","The 1900 household places him with Matthew and Lena.","The original 1900 census records 17-year-old Ferdinand in the Wilkes-Barre home of Matthew and Lena Kramer. Later records follow an older Ferdinand through rope work and a 1940 owned home.","His precise birth date and the Matthew/Matthias identity need original-record comparison.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)"],[["Original 1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/kramer-matthew-lena-census-1900.jpg"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("kramer-nine-living-1900",1900,"1900 census","Lena reports nine living children","kramer","Family","recorded","Two censuses name a plausible nine-child group across twenty years.","The 1900 Wilkes-Barre sheet says Lena bore eleven children, nine living, and names six at home. The 1880 household names three older children absent in 1900, plus Julia and Mary on both sheets. A compact visual reconstructs nine names; an 1898 directory suggests the six-year-old 1880 boy may be Marcus G.","The 1870 older daughters' names/ages conflict with 1880. The 1880 boy's name is unclear, and survival of all three older children to 1900 is probable but not proved. Two children who died before 1900 remain unnamed.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Martin / Matthew Kramer","Lena Kramer","nine possible living children"],[["Original 1880 census",ROOT+"sources/records/kramer-martin-lena-census-1880.jpg"],["Original 1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/kramer-matthew-lena-census-1900.jpg"],["Sibling guide",ROOT+"branches/kramer-eleven-children.md"]],"branches/kramer-eleven-children.md"),
  item("george-weatherford-1937",1937,"26 Oct 1937","A Danville certificate follows George's later work","weatherford","Death","recorded","The likely George Weatherford worked as a night watchman after his 1884 farmer entry.","The original 1937 certificate calls him G. W. Weatherford, age 76, wife Ella, father Tom, and night watchman at 824 Noble Street. Its informant Mrs. C. E. Cash matches the husband of Novella Weatherford Cash, whose 1951 certificate names George C. and Ella Lumpkin as parents and gives 824 Noble Avenue.","The initials G. W. and George C. conflict, although the shared wife, Cash link, age, origin and address make the match very strong. Neither record dates his occupational change or proves the Asa/Thomas father variants identical.","Danville, Virginia",["George C. Weatherford","Ella Lumpkin Weatherford","Novella Weatherford Cash"],[["1937 original certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/g-w-weatherford-death-certificate-1937.jpg"],["1951 daughter's certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/novella-weatherford-cash-death-certificate-1951.jpg"],["Identity diagram",ROOT+"maps/george-weatherford-record-bridge.svg"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("novella-weatherford-1951",1951,"28 Apr 1951","Novella Cash's certificate names both Weatherford parents","weatherford","Death","recorded","An original certificate adds a daughter to George and Ella's family.","Novella Weatherford Cash's death certificate reports birth 31 December 1887, parents George C. Weatherford and Ella Lumpkin, husband Carrington E. Cash and home at 824 Noble Avenue in Danville.","Her relation to Duffy is strongly inferred from two separate parent-naming certificates; a single household record naming both siblings has not been found.","Danville, Virginia",["Novella Weatherford Cash","George C. Weatherford","Ella Lumpkin Weatherford","Doctor Duffy Weatherford"],[["Original 1951 certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/novella-weatherford-cash-death-certificate-1951.jpg"],["Duffy's original certificate","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QVRZ-2C68?lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("weatherford-marriage",1884,"1884","George Weatherford and Ella Lumpkins marry","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","A Virginia register establishes this older Weatherford couple.","Their Pittsylvania County marriage is a record-backed step in Ashley's paternal Weatherford line. Son Doctor Duffy's later death certificate identifies this family generation.","George's parents conflict between records; no immigrant arrival is identified.","Pittsylvania County, Virginia",["George C. Weatherford","Ella Lumpkins"],[["Original marriage register",ROOT+"sources/records/george-ella-weatherford-marriage-register-1884.jpg"],["FamilySearch index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6NJ1-VGMH?lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("duffy-birth",1890,"1890","Doctor Duffy Weatherford is born","weatherford","Birth","recorded","His death certificate gives a North Carolina birthplace.","The 1966 original certificate reports Duffy's 1890 birth in Caswell County and names his parents. The 1930 census later places his young son Garnett in the family home.","Birth information on a death certificate came from an informant; seek a contemporary birth record.","Caswell County, North Carolina",["Doctor Duffy Weatherford"],[["Original death certificate","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C95D-9HD1?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3AQVRZ-2C68&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("ferdinand-louis-birth",1913,"24 Aug 1913","Ferdinand Louis Kramer is born","kramer","Birth","recorded","The state index names a Bosch mother.","Pennsylvania's birth index records Ferdinand Kramer in Wilkes-Barre on this date, mother ‘Bosch R.’ A 1930 household places Ferdinand L. with parents Ferdinand and Rose A. and brother Emil.","The index omits middle name and father; his direct link to Fred's later obituary would benefit from a full certificate.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Ferdinand Louis Kramer (1913)"],[["1913 state birth index","https://www.pa.gov/content/dam/copapwp-pagov/en/phmc/documents/archives/research-online/documents/1913-K.PDF#page=323"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("emil-birth",1915,"21 Dec 1915","Emil Carl Kramer is born","kramer","Birth","recorded","He later became a rope strander, machinist and Army serviceman.","Emil's signed 1940 draft card gives his birth and father Ferdinand; his 1941 marriage form names mother Rose Bosch. He was Ferdinand Louis's brother in the 1930 census.","His obituary reports wartime awards, but the military unit and precise awards still need a service record.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Emil Carl Kramer"],[["Signed draft card","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSW4-4QGH-W?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3AQ2SF-63JK&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("tancy-marriage",1918,"1918","William E. Blevins marries Tancy B. Barker","smith","Marriage","recorded","The name Tancy persists in Ashley's family memory.","A Sullivan County register records this marriage. Justin and Ashley recognize Tancy Blanche as a likely namesake of Ashley's sister.","William Howard Blevins's proposed parent-child link to William E. is not yet proved, so the exact namesake relationship is open.","Sullivan County, Tennessee",["William Elkanah Blevins","Tancy Blanche Barker"],[["Marriage register index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QKH7-PCBN?lang=en"]],"branches/blevins-deep-lineage.md"),
  item("mabel-birth-index-1919",1919,"31 Jan 1919","Mabel Reese is entered in Pennsylvania's birth index","miller","Birth","recorded","Her name, birthday and county match Pearl's later family account.","The original state index lists Mabel Reese, Lycoming County, 31 January 1919, certificate 53653. The date and county match Paulene's memoir and Pearl's later funeral program.","The mother field is faint and transcribed by MyHeritage as ‘M. Catteman,’ unlike Corderman. The index does not name a father or establish that this Mabel later used Velma Pearl Wilson. Obtain full certificate 53653.","Lycoming County, Pennsylvania; town not indexed",["Mabel Reese, strong candidate for Pearl Miller"],[["Original state birth index",ROOT+"sources/records/mabel-reese-pa-birth-index-1919.png"],["Pennsylvania index PDF","https://www.pa.gov/content/dam/copapwp-pagov/en/phmc/documents/archives/research-online/documents/1919-R.pdf#page=88"],["MyHeritage transcription","https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-21058-3011731/mabel-reese-in-pennsylvania-birth-index"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#the-1919-disruption"),
  item("ada-mabel",1919,"12 Feb 1919","Meda Reese dies twelve days after Mabel's indexed birth","miller","Death","family","The dates are indexed by the state; the parent-child relationship comes from family accounts.","Pennsylvania's original death index lists Meda Reese in Lycoming County on 12 February 1919, certificate 21836. Its timing follows Mabel Reese's indexed 31 January birth by twelve days. Paulene Miller Beach said Mabel's birth mother Ada died about two weeks after her birth and Wilson relatives later raised her.","The indexes do not prove Meda was Mabel's mother or give her maiden name or cause. The birth-index mother field is transcribed ‘M. Catteman,’ unlike Corderman. Today's Muncy Valley is in Sullivan County; the indexed birth county is Lycoming. Paulene said influenza; a memorial says pneumonia. Full birth and death certificates and Wilson guardianship records are needed.","Lycoming County, Pennsylvania; Muncy Valley town reported",["Meda/Ada Corderman Reese, proposed match","Mabel Louella Reese / Velma Pearl Wilson, proposed match"],[["Original state death index",ROOT+"sources/records/meda-reese-pa-death-index-1919.png"],["Original state birth index",ROOT+"sources/records/mabel-reese-pa-birth-index-1919.png"],["Paulene Miller Beach memoir","https://bhaven.org/paulene/life-journey"],["Place-sequence map",ROOT+"context/family-place-sequences.md"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#the-1919-disruption"),
  item("grace-reese-obituary-1997",1997,"22 Aug 1997 obituary index","Grace's obituary names her sister Pearl","miller","Research","proposed","A later family notice joins Pearl Miller to William and Ada Reese.","A transcription and derived index for Grace E. Reese Smith's obituary name parents William H. and Ada A. Reese, and sisters Pearl Miller, Hazel Lechler and Ruth Ritter. It independently supports Paulene's sibling list.","The printed newspaper original has not been read, and Pearl's own birth and Wilson guardianship records are still missing.","Muncy Valley / Sunbury, Pennsylvania",["Grace E. Reese Smith","Pearl Miller","William H. Reese","Ada A. Reese"],[["Obituary transcription and index","https://www.our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/lundahlbower/p177.htm"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#the-1919-disruption"),
  item("paulene-name-1941",1941,"7 Mar 1941, memoir date","Paulene Miller gets a name meant for Paul","miller","Birth","family","The younger Paul Miller inherited the name his parents had first chosen for her.","In her memoir, Paulene says Lester and Mabel expected a boy and planned to name the baby Paul. When she arrived, they adapted it to Paulene; she joked that her younger brother Paul was the one she was named after. She also recalled her father ice skating while they waited for the midwife.","Paulene's date, the doctor's reported misspelling and the birth scene come from her later account; compare her birth certificate before treating them as independently verified.","Berwick, Pennsylvania",["Paulene Fay Miller Beach","Paul Miller","Lester Edmond Miller","Mabel/Pearl Reese Wilson Miller"],[["Paulene's Life Journey","https://bhaven.org/paulene/life-journey"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#small-stories-paulene-preserved"),
  item("miller-homestead-1954",1954,"1954, memoir account","The Miller homestead is reportedly sold","miller","Home","family","Paulene remembers a $1,500 sale after Mary Gower Miller's death.","Paulene says that after grandmother Mary died, other heirs sold the Berwick house for $1,500 and Lester's household received no proceeds despite caring for her. Paulene and Marqueen then pooled babysitting money to buy their parents a dinette set for the next apartment.","The price, legal ownership and distribution need the deed and estate file. A reported sale is not proof of family net worth or a lost fortune.","Berwick, Pennsylvania",["Mary Emma Gower Miller","Lester Edmond Miller","Mabel/Pearl Reese Wilson Miller","Paulene Miller Beach","Marqueen Miller"],[["Paulene's Life Journey","https://bhaven.org/paulene/life-journey"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#small-stories-paulene-preserved"),
  item("cordaro-photo-estimate",1925,"c. 1915–1935, visual estimate","An unidentified Cordaro family portrait","cordaro","Research","proposed","A studio portrait may date to the 1920s, but no original date is known.","Justin identifies the image as Cordaro family and guessed the 1930s. Formal dress, the pale bow or collar, and a child's short trousers fit a broad early-twentieth-century period. A 1920s center is only a low-confidence visual guess.","The people, location and exact date are unknown. Image wear cannot date the exposure, and no evidence places it in Sutera or identifies the passport holder.","Location unknown",["Unidentified Cordaro family group"],[["Family photograph",ROOT+"sources/family/cordaro-family-portrait-unidentified.jpg"],["Sutera map",ROOT+"maps/cordaro-sutera-places.svg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("garnett-birth",1927,"8 Mar 1927","Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr. is born","weatherford","Birth","recorded","His own registration card gives a Pittsylvania County birthplace.","The signed 1946 card gives his date and an honorable Navy discharge; the 1930 census places him with parents Doctor Duffy and Annie.","A later obituary says Danville rather than Pittsylvania County; these may describe nearby places, but the exact birthplace needs a birth record.","Pittsylvania County, Virginia",["Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr."],[["Original 1946 card","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSZX-KJ56?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3AQ2W5-YQRZ&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("weatherford-household-1930",1930,"Apr 1930","Ten children in Duffy and Annie's Virginia household","weatherford","Home","recorded","A saved census page gives a family portrait in names, ages, work and a reported home value.","In Pittsylvania County's Dan River District, the census lists Duffy and Annie with children Thelma, Lillian, Mary E., John C., George, a son whose name is hard to read, James E., Junius, Garnett and William R. Duffy reports building carpentry and an owned home valued at $1,000.","The value is a single 1930 census estimate, not net worth. Some census names have only probable matches to later obituaries. Helen and any other younger children were not yet born.","Dan River District, Pittsylvania County, Virginia",["Doctor Duffy Weatherford","Annie Lillian Neathery Weatherford","Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr.","George Ira Weatherford"],[["Saved original census",ROOT+"sources/records/duffy-annie-weatherford-census-1930.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33S7-9RZF-DP2?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3ACFG1-7N2&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford-smith-generations.md#the-household-when-garnett-was-three"),
  item("weatherford-household-1940",1940,"10 Apr 1940","A second Weatherford census names younger siblings","weatherford","Home","recorded","Helen, Lindsay and Betty appear beside Garnett on the saved original page.","Duffy and Annie's 1940 Dan River District home includes James, a son written Junior/Junius, Garnett, William, Helen, Lindsay and Betty. Duffy reports city-cleaner work, $1,078 wages in 1939 and an owned home valued at $1,000.","This is one income year and one home valuation, not net worth or a ten-year earnings history. The formal name of Junior/Junius and his link to the later D.D. Jr. obituary name need confirmation.","Dan River District, Pittsylvania County, Virginia",["Doctor Duffy Weatherford","Annie Lillian Neathery Weatherford","Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr.","Helen Weatherford Barksdale"],[["Saved original census",ROOT+"sources/records/duffy-annie-weatherford-census-1940.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-89MR-3S4F?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AVRBR-3FX&action=view&cc=2000219&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford-smith-generations.md#the-household-when-garnett-was-three"),
  item("danville-mill-workers-1911",1911,"Jun 1911","A photographer records Danville mill workers","context","History","context","The period image makes the labor setting near the later Weatherford household visible.","Lewis Hine photographed Riverside Cotton Mills workers in Danville. His caption says he saw some workers who appeared under fourteen, despite the superintendent's denial. Textile mills and tobacco shaped the nearby city's economy while Duffy and Annie's children grew up.","The image predates the 1930 household and does not show a Weatherford or establish that any family member attended or missed school because of mill work.","Danville, Virginia",["Weatherford family, period context only"],[["Saved LOC photograph",ROOT+"media/danville-riverside-mill-workers-1911.jpg"],["Library of Congress catalog","https://www.loc.gov/item/2018674865/"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("blevins-hines-marriage",1931,"27 Aug 1931","William Howard Blevins marries Mary Elizabeth Hines","smith","Marriage","recorded","A Bristol-area couple later appears in Gladys's family records.","The original Sullivan County license names William H. Blevins and Mary E. Hines. Their 1940 household includes daughter Gladys, later Alice Smith's mother.","The license does not name William's parents; his proposed William Elkanah parentage remains open.","Sullivan County, Tennessee",["William Howard Blevins","Mary Elizabeth Hines"],[["Original marriage-license index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QKH7-PC1M?lang=en"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("cordaro-caucci-marriage",1938,"30 Apr 1938","Joseph Cordaro and Dena Caucci marry","cordaro","Marriage","recorded","Their application names all four parents.","Joseph, born in West Wyoming, worked as a laborer. Dena was born in Jessup. The original county form names Joseph's parents Anthony Cordaro and Petra Magro, and Dena's parents Joseph Caucci and Anna Belardinelli.","Handwritten surname spellings vary in the index. This record does not name Patricia's reported sisters.","Jessup, Pennsylvania",["Joseph Cordaro","Dena / Dina Caucci","Anthony Cordaro","Pietra Magro","Joseph Caucci","Anna Belardinelli"],[["Original county application",ROOT+"sources/records/cordaro-caucci-marriage-1938.jpg"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("fred-birth",1939,"8 Jul 1939","Fred / Ferdinand Francis Kramer is born","kramer","Birth","recorded","His obituary names both parents.","Fred's obituary gives Wilkes-Barre birth and names Ferdinand Louis Kramer and Mary Andrews. A 1950 census places a son Ferdinand with the matching household.","The 1950 enumerator's age differs by one year; use Fred's precise obituary date with that caveat.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Fred / Ferdinand Francis Kramer (1939)"],[["Fred's obituary","https://www.legacy.com/person/ferdinand-francis-kramer-%28fred%29-52588560"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("patricia-birth",1940,"9 Jun 1940","Patricia Cordaro is born in Kingston","cordaro","Birth","recorded","Her later marriage application gives the date, birthplace and both parents.","Patricia's 1962 Luzerne County marriage form reports Joseph Cordaro and Dina Caucci as her parents. Joseph and Dina were living in nearby West Wyoming in the 1940 census before her birth.","The birth date is an applicant statement on a later record; a birth or baptism certificate would independently verify it.","Kingston, Luzerne County, Pennsylvania",["Patricia Cordaro","Joseph Cordaro","Dina Caucci"],[["Luzerne County marriage archive","https://www.searchiqs.com/paluze/"],["1940 census","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KQ7W-L64?lang=en"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("cordaro-kramer-marriage",1962,"22 Nov 1962","Patricia Cordaro marries Fred Kramer","cordaro","Marriage","recorded","The original county application identifies her parents.","Patricia, a secretary, married Fred, a shoe-factory employee, in West Wyoming. Her application names Joseph Cordaro and Dina Caucci. Find Marriages book 191, page 2785 in the Luzerne County archive.","A paid download is optional; the county image can be viewed free. The record does not establish a link to the Sutera passport holder.","West Wyoming, Pennsylvania",["Patricia Cordaro","Fred / Ferdinand Francis Kramer (1939)","Joseph Cordaro","Dina Caucci"],[["Luzerne County marriage archive","https://www.searchiqs.com/paluze/"],["Archive instructions","https://www.luzernecounty.org/614/Register-of-Wills"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("cordaro-kramer-obit",2023,"12 Aug 2023","Patricia Cordaro's Kramer connection is named","cordaro","Research","recorded","Fred Kramer's obituary names wife Patricia Cordaro and son Paul.","The indexed Times Leader obituary gives Patricia Anne Marie Cordaro Kramer as Fred's wife and Paul Kramer among their children. Justin identifies Paul as his father and names Joan, Mary and Toni Cordaro as Patricia's sisters.","Patricia's parents are not named in this obituary; neither her birth/death status nor the passport holder's relationship is established.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Patricia Anne Marie Cordaro Kramer","Ferdinand Francis Kramer","Paul Kramer","Joan Cordaro","Mary Cordaro","Toni Cordaro"],[["Obituary index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6L83-L29V?lang=en"],["Family account",ROOT+"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"]],"branches/cordaro-passport-lead.md"),
  item("kramer-1940-work",1940,"1940 census; 1939 wages","Ferdinand’s rope work and home are recorded","kramer","Work","recorded","A concrete, limited economic snapshot.","The older Ferdinand reported 52 weeks as a wire-rope foreman and $1,996 in 1939 wages, plus an owned home estimated at $2,400. A resident son worked as a wire-rope winder and reported $1,234; daughter Hilda attended school.","Census wages and a home estimate are not household net worth or proof of inherited assets.","146 Prospect Street, Wilkes-Barre",["Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)","Emil Carl Kramer","Hilda Kramer"],[["Original 1940 census","https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/census-1940/T627/PA/m-t0627-03563/m-t0627-03563-00103.jpg"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("emil-1940-draft",1940,"16 Oct 1940","Emil Carl Kramer signs his draft card","kramer","Military","recorded","The two-sided card gives a signature, family contact and employer.","Emil registered while living at 146 Prospect Street. He gave his father Ferdinand as contact and American Chain & Cable as employer. The saved image includes both sides of the card.","Registration did not mean he had entered military service; an Army index records later enlistment in 1943.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Emil Carl Kramer","Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)"],[["Saved signed card",ROOT+"sources/records/emil-carl-kramer-draft-registration-1940.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSW4-4QGH-W?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AQ2SF-63JK&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("emil-victoria-1941",1941,"12 Feb 1941","Emil Kramer marries Victoria Giampietro","kramer","Marriage","recorded","A signed application identifies his parents and rope-factory job.","The right page of the saved Luzerne County application names Emil's parents Ferdinand and Rose Bosch and calls him a strander. It names Victoria Giampietro as his bride.","The left page belongs to another couple; read only the right page for Emil and Victoria.","Luzerne County, Pennsylvania",["Emil Carl Kramer","Victoria Giampietro","Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)","Rose Bosch Kramer"],[["Saved application",ROOT+"sources/records/emil-kramer-victoria-giampietro-marriage-1941.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33S7-9PR4-97SS?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AKHFH-X3X&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("emil-army",1943,"27 Jul 1943","Emil Carl Kramer enters the Army","kramer","Military","recorded","An enlistment index confirms wartime entry.","Emil's 1940 draft card named American Chain & Cable as his employer; his 1941 marriage called him a strander. The 1943 Army enlistment index establishes entry as a selectee/private.","The index does not establish his unit, overseas service or medals.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Emil Carl Kramer"],[["Army enlistment index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:K8TZ-8YY?lang=en"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("garnett-navy",1946,"By 1946","Garnett’s Navy discharge is recorded","weatherford","Military","recorded","His card says ‘Honorable Discharge Navy.’","Garnett's registration card confirms a Navy discharge by 1946. His later obituary reports service in the Pacific during World War II. Justin says Garnett joined at 17 after misstating his age; that detail is a family account.","The records found do not establish an enlistment date or declared age, ship, rank, Pacific stations or battles.","Virginia / Pacific theater, exact station unknown",["Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr."],[["Original 1946 card","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSZX-KJ56?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3AQ2W5-YQRZ&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("kramer-1950-homes",1950,"1950 census","Two Ferdinand households appear separately","kramer","Work","recorded","The younger worked at a wire-rope works; the elder was widowed.","The younger Ferdinand lived with wife Mary, sons Fred and Paul, and father-in-law Frank Andrews; he was a machine operator. The elder lived with daughter Hilda and son-in-law Joseph Wolosz.","The sampled census does not provide a whole-family wealth figure or explain property transfer.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)","Ferdinand Louis Kramer (1913)","Mary Andrews Kramer"],[["Younger household","https://1950census.archives.gov/iiif/2/1950census%2F43290879-Pennsylvania%2F43290879-Pennsylvania-135713%2F43290879-Pennsylvania-135713-0005.jpg/full/full/0/default.jpg"],["Elder household","https://1950census.archives.gov/iiif/2/1950census%2F43290879-Pennsylvania%2F43290879-Pennsylvania-135659%2F43290879-Pennsylvania-135659-0014.jpg/full/full/0/default.jpg"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("older-ferdinand-1951",1951,"5 Jun 1951","Ferdinand Kramer, the older foreman, dies","kramer","Death","recorded","His certificate links his rope work to parents named by the informant.","The Pennsylvania certificate identifies Ferdinand, age 68, as a retired wire-rope foreman. Informant Emil C. Kramer gave father Matthew Kramer and mother ‘Matilda Dasque.’ The same image is misfiled on the FamilySearch profile of his son Ferdinand Louis.","‘Matilda Dasque’ conflicts with the chart's ‘Magdalena Disque’ and census ‘Lena’; do not merge these names without earlier records.","Danville, Pennsylvania",["Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)","Emil Carl Kramer","Matthew Kramer","Matilda Dasque, as written"],[["Saved death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/ferdinand-kramer-1882-death-certificate-1951.jpg"],["FamilySearch memory","https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/122055960"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("roscoe-helen-1920",1920,"25 May 1920","Roscoe Morrison marries in Detroit","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","An earlier marriage names Florence's paternal great-grandparents.","Roscoe Gordon Morrison, an Indiana-born truck driver, married Helen Margaret Hanyon. The register names his parents Porter J. and Celia Morrison, matching the later Virginia death certificate. His reported age 19 conflicts with the later 1903 birth year.","Helen's surname handwriting needs a second reading. The marriage's ending and Roscoe's later marriage to Eleanor have not been found.","Detroit, Michigan",["Roscoe Gordon Morrison","Porter J. Morrison","Celia Morrison","Helen Margaret Hanyon"],[["Original marriage spread",ROOT+"sources/records/roscoe-helen-marriage-1920.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HT-6Q13-GV7?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AN313-C6T&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("morrison-bennett-marriage-1865",1865,"31 Aug 1865","Thomas Morrison marries Isaphinia Bennett","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","A Gibson County register supplies the Bennett surname for the probable earlier Morrison household.","The original Gibson County register, entry 1258, names Thomas Morrison and Isaphinia Bennett and records their license and marriage on 31 August 1865. A 1920 indexed marriage of daughter Lilly Florence independently calls her parents William Thomas Morrison and Jsophena Bennett.","The 1920 marriage image is available only at a FamilySearch Center or affiliate library. The couple is a strong match to the 1900–10 household, but their exact link through Roscoe's mother still needs a birth or parent-naming record.","Gibson County, Indiana",["Thomas / William Thomas Morrison","Isaphinia / Isophena Bennett Morrison","Lilly Florence Morrison Prince"],[["Original 1865 register",ROOT+"sources/records/thomas-morrison-isaphinia-bennett-marriage-1865.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:939N-T1SQ-4Y?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AVH4K-8G5&action=view&lang=en"],["1920 daughter index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:4135-KCMM?lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("morrison-indiana-1900",1900,"1900–10","An Indiana census may show Roscoe's grandparents","weatherford","Home","probable","A grandson's household points one generation beyond Celia Morrison.","The 1900 census places Thomas and Isophena Morrison with daughters Delia and Florence in Gibson County, Indiana. In 1910 Tom and Isophenia have daughters indexed Celia and Florence at matching ages, plus seven-year-old grandson Roscoe. Roscoe Gordon Morrison's 1920 marriage return names Celia Morrison as his mother.","The Delia/Celia name reading differs, and the 1910 census labels Roscoe a grandson without identifying which daughter was his mother. His birth record and father Porter J. Morrison's earlier household are still needed before calling Thomas and Isophena proved ancestors.","Gibson County, Indiana",["Thomas Morrison, probable ancestor","Isophena Morrison, probable ancestor","Delia/Celia Morrison, candidate","Roscoe Morrison, candidate"],[["1900 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/morrison-gibson-census-1900-candidate.jpg"],["1910 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/roscoe-morrison-candidate-census-1910.jpg"],["1920 Roscoe marriage",ROOT+"sources/records/roscoe-helen-marriage-1920.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("celia-birth-index-1886",1886,"21 Feb 1886, indexed","An unnamed Morrison girl has Celia's birthday","weatherford","Birth","probable","The same date and parent pair appear in Celia's later death certificate.","A Gibson County birth index records an unnamed female Morrison born in Washington Township on 21 February 1886, father Thos. Morrison, mother surnamed Bennett. Celia Melissa Capehart's 1977 original death certificate reports exactly that day and parents William Thomas Morrison and Isophena Bennett. The match is strong across independent records.","The birth index gives no first name and its original register image is unavailable online. Neither source names Roscoe as the girl's later son.","Washington Township, Gibson County, Indiana",["Unnamed Morrison girl, probable Celia Melissa","Thomas Morrison","Isophena Bennett Morrison"],[["1886 FamilySearch index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:4KTP-Q2T2?lang=en"],["1977 original certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/celia-melissa-morrison-capehart-death-1977.jpg"],["Identity diagram",ROOT+"maps/celia-morrison-identity-1886-1977.svg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("celia-denbo-marriage-1914",1914,"25 Aug 1914","Celia Morrison marries William Denbo","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","The original Gibson County register adds a named chapter to Celia's life.","The right page of the original county register, page 155, names William Denbo and Celia Morrison. The license and minister-certified ceremony are both dated 25 August 1914. Celia later appeared as Celia Melissa Denbo in a 1923 marriage index with her Morrison and Bennett parents named.","The 1914 entry gives no ages, parents, children or previous marital statuses. It neither proves nor rules out an earlier marriage to Roscoe's reported father Porter J. Morrison.","Gibson County, Indiana",["Celia Morrison","William Denbo","Porter J. Morrison, unresolved earlier link"],[["Original 1914 register",ROOT+"sources/records/celia-morrison-william-denbo-marriage-1914.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:939N-T1K3-V?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AVH42-2Z4&action=view&cc=1410397&lang=en"],["1923 marriage index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:4YZ7-3HW2?lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("celia-morrison-parentage",1923,"1923–77","Two later records name Celia Morrison's parents","weatherford","Research","recorded","The parent names strongly connect Delia or Celia to Thomas Morrison and Isophena Bennett.","Celia Melissa Denbo's 1923 Indiana marriage index names Thomas Morrison and Isophena Bennett. Her original 1977 Virginia death certificate gives William Thomas Morrison and Isophena Bennett, with Indiana birth in 1886. The 1900 Delia and 1910 Celia have matching ages in that parents' Gibson County household. A 1950 original census places Celia Capehart with her husband and Denbo son in Fairfax County.","The 1923 original image was restricted. None of Celia's marriage, census or death records names Roscoe as her son. That generation link remains probable until a birth or explicit mother-and-son record is found.","Gibson County, Indiana → Fairfax County, Virginia",["Celia Melissa Morrison Denbo Capehart","William Thomas Morrison","Isophena Bennett Morrison","Roscoe Gordon Morrison, possible son"],[["1923 marriage index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:4YZ7-3HW2?lang=en"],["1977 original death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/celia-melissa-morrison-capehart-death-1977.jpg"],["1950 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/capehart-denbo-household-census-1950.jpg"],["Identity diagram",ROOT+"maps/celia-morrison-identity-1886-1977.svg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("celia-capehart-fairfax-1950",1950,"9 May 1950","Celia Capehart's household is in Fairfax","weatherford","Migration","recorded","A possible earlier Morrison relative appears in northern Virginia.","The original Mount Vernon census has Indiana-born Celia M. Capehart, 64, with husband George and his stepson William C. Denbo, William's wife and child. George and William are sheet-metal workers. A 1930 census index had Celia in Gibson County, Indiana, so the move occurred sometime between those censuses.","The census supplies no move date or motive. Roscoe also worked sheet metal in northern Virginia, but no joint employer or work relationship is documented. Celia's exact mother-and-son link to Roscoe is still unproved.","Gibson County, Indiana → Fairfax County, Virginia",["Celia Melissa Morrison Denbo Capehart","George Capehart","William C. Denbo","Roscoe Gordon Morrison, possible son"],[["1950 original census",ROOT+"sources/records/capehart-denbo-household-census-1950.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QHK-9QHW-7LFG?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A6XL5-C949&action=view&cc=4464515&lang=en"],["1930 index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:XHMN-1M9?lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("morrison-birth-candidate-1903",1903,"20 Feb 1903, indexed","An unnamed Gibson County infant may be Roscoe","weatherford","Birth","proposed","The birth index matches Roscoe's 1910 township and nearly matches his later reported birthday.","A Gibson County birth-register index lists an unnamed male Morrison born in Washington Township on 20 February 1903. It indexes his mother as Clair Morrison and his father as Unknown. The source is film 549364, digital group 7578554, page 27, line 6.","Roscoe's signed 1942 card gives 19 February in Hazleton, a town Indiana maps in White River Township; the birth index says Washington Township. His later records name Celia and Porter as parents. The original birth page is restricted to a FamilySearch Center or affiliate library, so this child cannot yet be identified as Roscoe or Clair as Celia/Delia.","Washington Township, Gibson County, Indiana",["Unnamed Morrison boy, candidate","Clair Morrison, indexed mother","Roscoe Gordon Morrison, possible match"],[["FamilySearch birth index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:4KY3-2PPZ?lang=en"],["Birth-volume catalog","https://www.familysearch.org/en/search/catalog/koha:132328"],["Indiana township map","https://www.in.gov/dlgf/files/2026-reports/Gibson-County-2025-Land-Order.pdf"],["Roscoe's signed draft card",ROOT+"sources/records/roscoe-gordon-morrison-draft-card-1942.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#eleanors-earlier-household-a-strong-still-testable-bridge"),
  item("roscoe-candidate-1930",1930,"12 Apr 1930","A likely Roscoe Morrison works at a steel mill","weatherford","Work","probable","A pre-marriage snapshot places a Roscoe near Eleanor's Verona home.","An original Jefferson Township census sheet lists a 27-year-old Indiana-born Roscoe Morrison as single, a steel-mill laborer, and cousin in Withe Briscoe's household. The name, age, birthplace and Allegheny location closely fit Florence's father. Eleanor's separate 1930 census lists her with widowed mother Louisa in Verona.","No middle name or parents are recorded for this Roscoe, so the identity and Briscoe relationship need proof. The census marital-status entry does not establish when his earlier marriage legally ended or when he married Eleanor.","Jefferson Township, Allegheny County, Pennsylvania",["Roscoe Morrison, likely Florence's father","Eleanor M. Hollinger","Withe Briscoe"],[["Original 1930 census",ROOT+"sources/records/roscoe-morrison-candidate-census-1930.jpg"],["FamilySearch original","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33SQ-GRCS-D9T?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AXCHK-HHN&action=view&cc=1810731&lang=en"],["Eleanor's separate census",ROOT+"sources/records/hollinger-candidate-census-1930.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("briscoe-patent-1946",1946,"28 May 1946","A possible Morrison cousin patents a safety device","weatherford","Work","probable","Withe Briscoe's patent gives the 1930 household an industrial story.","The U.S. patent names Withe Briscoe of Elizabeth, Pennsylvania as inventor of a device to protect liquid-sealed gas-pressure controls from sudden pressure changes. A Withe and Callie Briscoe pair is buried near Elizabeth, strongly matching the 1930 household where a likely Roscoe Morrison was called the head's cousin.","The census cousin relationship has not been proved through parents; Roscoe is not named on the patent. No employer, sale, family income, or inheritance is established.","Elizabeth, Allegheny County, Pennsylvania",["Withe Briscoe","Roscoe Morrison, likely Florence's father"],[["Patent drawing",ROOT+"sources/records/withe-briscoe-patent-drawing-1946.png"],["Complete patent",ROOT+"sources/records/withe-briscoe-patent-1946.pdf"],["Patent index","https://patents.google.com/patent/US2401046A/en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md#a-possible-cousins-invention"),
  item("hollinger-1900",1900,"1900 census","Linaus and Louisa Hollinger in Pittsburgh","weatherford","Work","recorded","A new household and iron-mill job appear one year after their marriage.","The original census records Pennsylvania-born Linaus Hollinger, an iron-mill laborer, with German-born wife Louisa and newborn son Theodore. Louisa reported an 1889 arrival. A 1941 death notice later names their sons Theodore and Paul and daughter Eleanor Morrison, strongly linking this home to Florence's mother.","The 1900 arrival year is a census report, not a passenger record. Louisa's birthplace conflicts across the marriage docket, later censuses and memorial; the 1941 notice has been read only in transcription.","Pittsburgh, Allegheny County, Pennsylvania",["Linaus P. Hollinger","Louisa E. Straub Hollinger","Theodore Hollinger"],[["Original 1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/hollinger-household-census-1900.jpg"],["1941 notice transcription","https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/139916983/louisa_e-hollinger"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("hollinger-candidate-1930",1930,"1930 census","Eleanor M. Hollinger in Verona","weatherford","Home","recorded","A later death notice names Louisa's married daughter Eleanor Morrison.","The Verona census records widowed Louisa E. Hollinger with unmarried daughter Eleanor M., 20. In 1941, Louisa's death notice named daughter Eleanor Morrison and sons Theodore and Paul, strongly connecting this family to Florence's mother. The 1930 sheet appears to report Louisa's arrival in 1887.","The 1941 notice has only been read in transcription. Louisa's 1900 arrival report says 1889 and the 1920 index says 1895; her birthplace also conflicts. An original arrival document is needed.","Verona, Allegheny County, Pennsylvania",["Eleanor M. Hollinger","Louisa E. Hollinger"],[["Original 1930 census",ROOT+"sources/records/hollinger-candidate-census-1930.jpg"],["1941 notice transcription","https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/139916983/louisa_e-hollinger"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("louisa-straub-birth-1877",1877,"19 Aug 1877, later report","Louisa Straub is reported born in France","weatherford","Birth","reported","Her 1941 certificate supplies the first original-record report of father Nicholas.","The Pennsylvania death certificate gives Louisa's birth date as 19 August 1877, birthplace France, and father Nicholas Straub, also France-born. The 1900 census independently gives August 1877 as her birth month.","The birth details came from a 1941 informant, not a contemporary register; three censuses say Germany and the 1899 marriage docket appears to say Pennsylvania. Her town of birth and mother are unknown.","France, per 1941 certificate; town unrecorded",["Louisa E. Straub Hollinger","Nicholas Straub"],[["Original death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/louisa-straub-hollinger-death-certificate-1941.jpg"],["1900 census",ROOT+"sources/records/hollinger-household-census-1900.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("louisa-hollinger-1941",1941,"20 Jun 1941","Louisa Hollinger dies; certificate names Nicholas Straub","weatherford","Death","recorded","Her certificate identifies her father; a notice names her married daughter.","Pennsylvania certificate 54334 records Louisa E. Hollinger's death in Verona, 19 August 1877 birth in France, father Nicholas Straub born in France, and about 50 years in the U.S. A separately transcribed Pittsburgh Press notice names daughter Eleanor Morrison and sons Theodore and Paul, matching the earlier Hollinger household.","The certificate's birthplace and residence duration depend on an informant; censuses report Germany and different arrival years. Its mother field says not known. The original newspaper image and a contemporary birth or passenger record remain uninspected.","Verona, Allegheny County, Pennsylvania",["Louisa E. Straub Hollinger","Nicholas Straub","Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison","Theodore W. Hollinger","Paul L. Hollinger"],[["Original death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/louisa-straub-hollinger-death-certificate-1941.jpg"],["FamilySearch memory","https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/175026156"],["Death notice transcription","https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/139916983/louisa_e-hollinger"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("morrison-1940",1940,"1940 census","Florence's childhood household in Arlington","weatherford","Home","recorded","Six children and Roscoe's sheet-metal work are on one sheet.","The Morrison household had come from Pennsylvania to Arlington by 1940. Roscoe was a sheet-metal worker; two-year-old Florence and newborn Catherine were Virginia-born, while four older children were Pennsylvania-born.","The census establishes residence and birth states, not a reason for moving or total family assets.","Arlington County, Virginia",["Roscoe Gordon Morrison","Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison","Florence Ruth Morrison"],[["Original 1940 census",ROOT+"sources/records/morrison-household-census-1940.jpg"],["FamilySearch", "https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VRY4-2Q8?lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("roscoe-morrison-death",1947,"2 Mar 1947","Roscoe Morrison dies at 44","weatherford","Death","recorded","Florence was about nine; Eleanor later headed the household.","Roscoe's Virginia death certificate calls him a sheet-metal contractor and names his parents Porter Morrison and Celia. Eleanor supplied the information. The family remained in northern Virginia after his death.","Porter and Celia's earlier records and Celia's surname remain unknown.","Fairfax County, Virginia",["Roscoe Gordon Morrison","Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison","Florence Ruth Morrison","Porter Morrison","Celia Morrison"],[["Original death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/roscoe-gordon-morrison-death-1947.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("morrison-1950",1950,"8 May 1950 census","Eleanor heads a seven-child Fairfax household","weatherford","Home","recorded","The census shows the family's situation three years after Roscoe's death.","Widowed Eleanor was listed as household head with Edwin, Celia, Paul, William, Florence, Catherine and Evelyn. Her work field says ‘washes clothes’ and ‘family work’; Edwin, 18, was a construction carpenter's helper and Paul, 15, a grocery clerk. Twelve-year-old Florence lived with them.","The form does not establish whether Eleanor's washing was paid employment or give wages for the family.","Providence District, Fairfax County, Virginia",["Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison","Florence Ruth Morrison","Edwin Wayne Morrison","Paul Frederick Morrison"],[["Original 1950 census",ROOT+"sources/records/morrison-household-census-1950.jpg"],["Evelyn on continuation",ROOT+"sources/records/morrison-household-census-1950-continuation.jpg"],["FamilySearch","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6XL5-KCHF?lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("garnett-florence",1954,"16 Jan 1954, ceremony","Garnett and Florence marry in Alexandria","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","The original names Florence's parents and both spouses' work.","The Fairfax certificate records a 16 January Alexandria ceremony after a 14 January license. It names Roscoe Gordon Morrison and Eleanor Mae Hollinger as Florence's parents, Florence as a First National Bank bookkeeper, and Garnett as a transit bus operator.","The groom is typed ‘Garrett’ on the original despite Garnett in other records. Florence's stated age 21 conflicts with her childhood censuses and obituary birth year.","Alexandria, Virginia",["Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr.","Florence Ruth Morrison","Roscoe Gordon Morrison","Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison"],[["Original marriage certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/garnett-florence-marriage-1954.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("eleanor-morrison-death",1968,"22 Nov 1968","Eleanor Morrison dies","weatherford","Death","recorded","A certificate gives Florence's mother's dates and lists Edwin as informant.","Eleanor's Virginia death certificate reports 13 June 1909 birth in Pennsylvania and 22 November 1968 death in Fairfax County. It calls her Roscoe's widow and names son Edwin as informant.","Her own parents are not identified on the certificate.","Fairfax County, Virginia",["Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison","Florence Ruth Morrison","Edwin Wayne Morrison"],[["Original death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/eleanor-mae-hollinger-morrison-death-1968.jpg"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("florence-weatherford-death",2019,"17 Feb 2019","Florence Morrison Weatherford dies","weatherford","Death","recorded","Her obituary connects Ashley to the older Morrison household.","The published obituary transcription gives Florence's parents as Rasco G. and Eleanor M. Hollinger Morrison, names her Weatherford children and grandchildren, and reports death on 17 February 2019. Its 20 May 1937 birth agrees in year with her childhood census ages.","The obituary's birth year conflicts with age 21 on the 1954 marriage certificate; the birth certificate has not been located.","Alexandria, Virginia",["Florence Ruth Morrison Weatherford","Roscoe Gordon Morrison","Eleanor Mae Hollinger Morrison","John Gordon Weatherford Sr.","Ashley Marie Weatherford"],[["2019 obituary transcription","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:61JY-8KNX?lang=en"]],"branches/morrison-hollinger.md"),
  item("smith-house-built",1960,"1960","The Miller Drive Smith house is built","smith","Home","recorded","The parcel later becomes a family landmark.","Fairfax County dates the house at 6330 Miller Drive to 1960. Alice's later marriage return records her at this address, and family account places her parents James and Gladys Smith there.","A construction year does not establish the Smiths' move-in date or who paid for the house.","Franconia, Fairfax County, Virginia",["James Allen Smith","Gladys Louise Blevins Smith"],[["Fairfax parcel lookup","https://icare.fairfaxcounty.gov/ffxcare/search/commonsearch.aspx?mode=address"]],"branches/weatherford-smith.md"),
  item("berwick-acf-closes",1962,"Early 1960s, approximate","Berwick's largest old factory closes","miller","History","context","The mill town of the Miller childhood changes.","A Columbia County Historical and Genealogical Society account says American Car & Foundry closed its Berwick operation in the early 1960s, with serious local economic effects. Its reprint of a 1902 newspaper story shows how strongly the earlier factory boom had shaped jobs, housing and land speculation.","1962 is only a plotting position within the reported early-1960s window. No Miller job at ACF or household loss from the closure has been documented.","Berwick, Pennsylvania",["Lester Edmond Miller","Mabel/Pearl Miller"],[["Local history and 1902 newspaper reprint","https://colcohist-gensoc.org/wp-content/uploads/BerwickBoom.pdf"],["Family economic context",ROOT+"context/family-in-its-times.md"]],"context/family-in-its-times.md"),
  item("duffy-death",1966,"1966","Doctor Duffy Weatherford dies","weatherford","Death","recorded","His certificate anchors an older Weatherford generation.","The original Virginia death certificate provides dates, place and reported parents. It distinguishes Duffy from other men named Doctor or D.D. Weatherford in derivative trees.","Informant-supplied birth facts still merit comparison with earlier records.","Danville, Virginia",["Doctor Duffy Weatherford"],[["Original death certificate","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C95D-9HD1?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3AQVRZ-2C68&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("phelps-milton-1880",1880,"1880 census","A school-age Phelps daughter in a Caswell farm household","weatherford","Home","recorded","The original page records the father's farming and a twelve-year-old daughter at school.","In Milton, Caswell County, R. O. Phelps, 45, was recorded as a farmer. Wife A. E. and eight children were listed; daughter A. E., 12, was marked at school. Names, age and place make this a strong candidate childhood home for the later A. L. Phelps bride.","The daughter's initials A. E. differ from the bride's A. L.; the census names no school and gives no acreage or family wealth.","Milton, Caswell County, North Carolina",["R. O. Phelps","A. E. Phelps","Annie Phelps"],[["Original census",ROOT+"sources/records/phelps-household-census-1880.jpg"],["Daughter index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MC6X-5J4?lang=en"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("phelps-estate-1885",1885,"1884–87","Ann Phelps auctions part of the estate after Robert's death","weatherford","Property","recorded","Original papers follow a widow's allotment, a December 1885 land auction and the later cash account.","Robert C. Phelps died in December 1884. Ann E. Phelps received personal property valued at $843.23 and estimated about $1,500 of debts. Her sale report records a 12 December 1885 Milton auction: three half-lot interests and 85 acres after her dower, with $1,562.50 in winning bids. The 1887 administrator account recorded $1,619.53 received and $1,672.87 paid, a $53.34 deficit. Tobacco already in Danville shows the farm's market connection.","The petitions estimate farm acreage differently, 200 versus 270 acres. Allotment, bids and cash flows cannot be combined into family net worth or an heir's inheritance. The 1885 daughter-name variants and 1889 bride's initials remain unresolved.","Milton, Caswell County, North Carolina",["Robert C. Phelps","Ann E. Foster Phelps","Phelps children"],[["1885 widow's allotment",ROOT+"sources/records/robert-phelps-widow-allotment-1885.jpg"],["1885 sale report, page 1",ROOT+"sources/records/robert-phelps-land-sale-report-1885-page1.jpg"],["1885 sale report, page 2",ROOT+"sources/records/robert-phelps-land-sale-report-1885-page2.jpg"],["1887 cash account",ROOT+"sources/records/robert-phelps-final-account-1887-page2.jpg"],["Accounting visual",ROOT+"maps/phelps-estate-1885.svg"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("phelps-neathery-marriage-1889",1889,"Early Feb 1889","John Neathery marries A. L. Phelps in Milton","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","The original register names bride, groom and both parent pairs.","The Caswell register's fifth row pairs J. R. C. Neathery with A. L. Phelps and lists their parents as James and Frances Neathery, and Robert and A. E. Phelps. It independently links Annie's reported parents before John's later marriage to Mary Moring.","The indexed groom is misread Mathery. The indexed day and shared-tree day differ; the bride's expanded first and middle names are absent from this register.","Milton, Caswell County, North Carolina",["John R. C. Neathery","A. L. Phelps","James Neathery","Frances Neathery","Robert Phelps","A. E. Phelps"],[["Original marriage register",ROOT+"sources/records/phelps-neathery-marriage-register-1889.jpg"],["Bride index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QPS9-PMC7?lang=en"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("neathery-moring-marriage-1894",1894,"26 Dec 1894","John Neathery marries Mary Moring after Annie's birth","weatherford","Marriage","recorded","A marriage index helps distinguish Annie's reported mother from her later stepmother.","A Prince Edward County marriage index pairs J. R. C. Neathery with Mary H. Moring in December 1894. Annie's later death certificate reports a March 1894 birth and names Annie Phelps as mother. In the 1900 census, Mary reports one child born and none living although Oscar and Annie live with her and John.","The marriage image requires a FamilySearch center or affiliate. The records strongly support Mary as stepmother but do not establish whether Annie Phelps died or separated from John.","Prince Edward County marriage; Danville household, Virginia",["John R. C. Neathery","Mary H. Moring","Annie L. Neathery","Annie Phelps","Oscar C. Neathery"],[["1894 marriage index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:68R4-QK7L?lang=en"],["1900 census sheet 2A",ROOT+"sources/records/neathery-household-census-1900-sheet2a.jpg"],["1900 continuation",ROOT+"sources/records/neathery-household-census-1900-sheet2b.jpg"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("neathery-danville-1900",1900,"2 Jun 1900","Annie and Oscar grow up in a Danville textile household","weatherford","Home","recorded","The original census spans two sheets and records work, schooling and rent.","John R. C. Neathery worked as a cotton mill weaver in Danville's Fourth Ward. The family rented its home. Oscar C., age eight, was recorded at school for nine months; six-year-old Annie L. was recorded for zero months that census year.","The census does not name a school or give wages. Zero months does not prove Annie never attended school; the 1911 Riverside mill photo depicts local context, not this family.","Danville Fourth Ward, Virginia",["John R. C. Neathery","Oscar C. Neathery","Annie L. Neathery","Mary H. Moring"],[["Original sheet 2A",ROOT+"sources/records/neathery-household-census-1900-sheet2a.jpg"],["Original sheet 2B",ROOT+"sources/records/neathery-household-census-1900-sheet2b.jpg"],["Period Danville mill photo",ROOT+"media/danville-riverside-mill-workers-1911.jpg"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("john-neathery-death-1953",1953,"12 Mar 1953","John Cabell Neathery's certificate names another generation","weatherford","Death","recorded","The original names James Neathery and Frances Wiles.","John Cabell Neathery's Virginia certificate names his wife Mary Moring, parents James Neathery and Frances Wiles, and retired textile work. The wife and trade fit Annie's father in the 1900 census.","His father is J. R. in an 1894 marriage index, while this certificate says James; his birth year also varies. The full Richard Cabell name appears in a tree, not on this certificate.","Danville, Virginia",["John Cabell Neathery","Mary H. Moring","James Neathery","Frances Wiles Neathery"],[["Original death certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/john-cabell-neathery-death-1953.jpg"],["FamilySearch viewer","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C95D-7W74?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AQVRD-QV3B&action=view&lang=en"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("oscar-neathery-mother-1945",1945,"21 Dec 1945","Oscar's certificate names Lillian Phelps","weatherford","Death","recorded","An original record identifies the brother's mother by name.","Oscar Cabell Netherly's Virginia death certificate names his parents John C. Netherly and Lillian Phelps, wife Callie Atkins, and textile work. The 1900 household calls him Oscar C. Neathery; his 1911 marriage index gives parents J. R. C. Neathery and Annie L. Neathery.","The name is spelled Netherly on the 1945 form. Its parent details are retrospective reports. Together with his sister Annie's 1969 certificate naming Annie Phelps, the evidence strongly supports Annie Lillian Phelps as their mother, but her own birth and death records remain unlocated.","Danville, Virginia",["Oscar Cabell Neathery","John C. Neathery","Annie Lillian Phelps","Callie Atkins Neathery"],[["Original 1945 certificate",ROOT+"sources/records/oscar-cabell-neathery-death-1945.jpg"],["1911 marriage index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:F8F4-KP5?lang=en"],["Family analysis",ROOT+"branches/neathery-phelps.md"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("annie-death",1969,"1969","Annie Neathery Weatherford dies","weatherford","Death","recorded","Her certificate names her parents.","Annie's original death certificate reports parents John R. Neathery and Annie Phelps. A 1900 census puts her with brother Oscar, father John, and his later wife Mary Moring.","An 1889 marriage register names A. L. Phelps; her proposed 1894 death remains unverified. Mary's census report of one child born and none living strongly points to a stepmother relationship; do not replace the certificate's mother with the index's household label.","Danville, Virginia",["Annie Neathery Weatherford","John R. Neathery","Annie Phelps","Mary H. Moring"],[["Original death certificate","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C95D-49MW-W?view=index&personArk=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3AQVYL-N4BR&action=view&lang=en"],["Family evidence",ROOT+"branches/neathery-phelps.md"]],"branches/neathery-phelps.md"),
  item("bridon-change",1972,"1972","Bridon acquires ACCO wire-rope assets","context","History","context","The Bridon-American name belongs to a later industrial chapter.","A government industry study dates Bridon's acquisition of American Chain & Cable rope assets to 1972. Emil's 1940 card names ACCO, and Ferdinand Louis's obituary reports a career at ACCO followed by Bridon Industries.","This company history and the younger son's obituary do not establish the older Ferdinand's employer.","Wilkes-Barre area, Pennsylvania",["Emil Carl Kramer"],[["Government industry study","https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2019/isde-ised/RG54-1-4-eng.pdf"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("ab-w-metro-1973",1973,"4 Feb 1973","Garnett's bus company becomes part of Metrobus","weatherford","Work","context","A transit merger gives a date to the employer change in his obituary.","WMATA's chronology dates its purchase of AB&W Transit to 4 February 1973. Garnett Sr.'s obituary reports 25 years driving for AB&W and 16 for Metro. The public event helps explain the two employer names in his career.","No personnel file establishes his own transfer date, route, earnings or whether the acquisition changed his duties.","Alexandria / Washington region",["Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr."],[["WMATA chronology","https://www.wmata.com/about/history/upload/history.pdf"],["Garnett obituary","https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/local/2007/07/03/obituaries/43ba782a-181c-4c59-a78e-7d361d86bf4a/"]],"context/family-in-its-times.md"),
  item("younger-ferdinand-1976",1976,"Mar 1976","Ferdinand Louis Kramer's obituary traces his career","kramer","Death","recorded","A family notice names his schools and a long rope-industry career.","The clipping reports G.A.R. High School, National Radio Institute classes in Washington, over 44 years at American Chain & Cable, later Bridon Industries, and retirement in 1975. It names wife Mary Andrews, sons Fred and Paul, brother Emil and sister Hilda.","The newspaper masthead is absent from the saved clipping; education and employment are family-reported, pending school and personnel records.","Wilkes-Barre area, Pennsylvania",["Ferdinand Louis Kramer (1913)","Mary Andrews Kramer","Fred Kramer","Paul Kramer","Emil Carl Kramer","Hilda Kramer Wolosz"],[["Saved obituary clipping",ROOT+"sources/records/ferdinand-louis-kramer-obituary-1976.jpg"],["FamilySearch memory","https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/122055961"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("mabel-paulene-cruise",1999,"Before 31 Jan 1999, based on Mabel's indexed birth date","Paul Miller sends Mabel and Paulene on a cruise","miller","Travel","family","Mabel later called it her favorite trip.","Paulene recalled that her brother Paul Miller, Justin's maternal grandfather, sent their mother Mabel/Pearl and Paulene on an island cruise before Mabel's 80th birthday. The family held a birthday party after they returned.","The 1999 bound is calculated from the 31 January 1919 birth-index entry and family account, not a dated travel record. The memoir does not name the ship, islands, departure date or how Paul arranged or paid for the trip.","Islands not specified",["Paul Miller","Mabel/Pearl Reese Wilson Miller","Paulene Miller Beach"],[["Paulene's first-person memoir","https://bhaven.org/paulene/life-journey"],["Mabel Reese birth index",ROOT+"sources/records/mabel-reese-pa-birth-index-1919.png"],["Family moments",ROOT+"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#small-stories-paulene-preserved"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#small-stories-paulene-preserved"),
  item("miller-family-band",1998,"Before 1998; date of performances unknown","Three relatives play in a Berwick country band","miller","Art","family","Lester's harmonica case and set lists survived.","Michael Beach remembers grandfather Lester on mandolin and harmonica, uncle Carl on drums, and father Fred on bass. Carl taught Michael drums. After Lester died, Pearl gave Michael Lester's case marked ‘Shorty,’ a stage belt and handwritten song lists photographed on Michael's page.","1998 is Lester's reported death year, not a documented performance year. The band roles and objects are Michael's first-person account; venues and recordings are not established.","Berwick area, Pennsylvania",["Lester Edmond Miller","Carl Miller","Fred Beach","Michael Beach"],[["Michael Beach's account and object photos","https://bhaven.org/mm-gang/harmonica-selections"],["Pearl's 2008 family tribute","https://bhaven.org/uploads/3/4/0/3/34038663/vol14sec2.pdf#page=1"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#small-stories-paulene-preserved"),
  item("miller-berwick-household-1950",1950,"1950 census","Five Miller children share a Berwick home","miller","Work","recorded","Two original census sheets reunite a family split by a page break.","At 1912 Fairview Avenue, widowed Mary E. Miller headed a household that included Lester and Velma, their children Marqueen, Paulene, Shirley, Paul and Carl, and other relatives. Lester reported quilling in a weaving mill; Velma did housework in a private home during the previous week.","The forms do not give annual earnings or net worth. They name the children as Mary's grandchildren; Carl's Social Security index independently names Lester and Velma as his parents. Paulene later recalled 1908 rather than 1912 Fairview Avenue.","Berwick, Pennsylvania",["Mary Emma Gower Miller","Lester E. Miller","Velma P. Wilson Miller","Carl Eugene Miller"],[["Original sheet 15",ROOT+"sources/records/miller-berwick-household-census-1950-sheet15.jpg"],["Original sheet 16",ROOT+"sources/records/miller-berwick-household-census-1950-sheet16.jpg"],["Carl's parent-naming index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6K9G-Y87C?lang=en"]],"branches/miller-berwick-timeline.md"),
  item("carl-miller-burial-lead",2001,"12 Feb 2001 in federal indexes; grave index differs","Carl Eugene Miller's identity is established","miller","Military","recorded","His Social Security application index names Lester and Velma as his parents.","Carl Eugene Miller's Social Security index gives 17 February 1948 birth in Berwick, names Lester E. Miller and Velma P. Wilson as parents, and reports death on 12 February 2001. Original 1950 census sheets place him with their family. A matching Berwick veterans' burial index describes a Carl E. Miller as Army SP4, Vietnam.","The military index itself lacks parents, unit, duty station and deployment dates. Its Vietnam tag does not prove in-country service or Agent Orange exposure. A derivative grave index instead gives 18 February death; seek an original certificate.","Berwick, Pennsylvania",["Carl Eugene Miller","Lester E. Miller","Velma P. Wilson Miller"],[["Parent-naming federal index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6K9G-Y87C?lang=en"],["1950 census sheet 15",ROOT+"sources/records/miller-berwick-household-census-1950-sheet15.jpg"],["1950 census sheet 16",ROOT+"sources/records/miller-berwick-household-census-1950-sheet16.jpg"],["Veterans' burial index","https://www.interment.net/united-states/pennsylvania/veterans-burials/records.php?page=1482"],["Exposure assessment",ROOT+"research/carl-miller-agent-orange.md"]],"research/carl-miller-agent-orange.md"),
  item("emil-2004",2004,"2004","Emil Carl Kramer's obituary recounts war and work","kramer","Death","recorded","The clipping reports a Purple Heart and decades as a machinist.","Emil's obituary says he served in the Army's Middle Eastern Theater, received a Purple Heart and ‘three Bronze Stars,’ and retired as an ACCO machinist after 36 years. It also names wife Victoria and sister Hilda.","The wording ‘Bronze Stars’ may mean medals or bronze campaign/service stars. A discharge or award record is needed to resolve that and confirm the unit and wounds.","Wilkes-Barre area, Pennsylvania",["Emil Carl Kramer","Victoria Giampietro Kramer","Hilda Kramer Wolosz"],[["Saved obituary clipping",ROOT+"sources/records/emil-carl-kramer-obituary-2004.jpg"],["FamilySearch memory","https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/122056249"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("pearl-miller-2008",2008,"7 Apr 2008","Pearl Miller's program preserves a portrait and family memories","miller","Death","recorded","Her grandson remembered a home full of cousins, music and travel.","A family funeral program gives Velma ‘Pearl’ Miller's birth and death as 31 January 1919 and 7 April 2008. Michael Beach recalled band practice with Lester, Carl and Fred, motorhome trips across the country, and a campsite bear she wanted to hug.","The program confirms her dates but does not identify her birth parents. The portrait has no exposure date; the bear anecdote is Michael's memory.","Berwick, Pennsylvania",["Velma Pearl Wilson Miller","Mabel Louella Reese, reported birth name","Michael Beach"],[["2008 program and tribute","https://bhaven.org/uploads/3/4/0/3/34038663/vol14sec2.pdf#page=1"]],"branches/miller-gower-reese.md#small-stories-paulene-preserved"),
  item("hilda-portrait-2020",2020,"2020 remembrance; photo undated","Hilda Kramer Wolosz in a family portrait","kramer","Research","family","A contributor-tagged image shows the older Ferdinand's daughter.","A FamilySearch memory uploaded in 2021 is tagged as Hilda M. Kramer Wolosz. Her obituary names parents Ferdinand and Rose Bosch Kramer and records her 2020 death. The portrait itself has no date.","The portrait identity is contributor-supplied. Its 2021 upload date and this 2020 timeline placement are not dates of exposure.","Place of exposure unknown",["Hilda M. Kramer Wolosz","Ferdinand Kramer (c. 1882)","Rose Bosch Kramer"],[["Saved family portrait",ROOT+"sources/family/hilda-kramer-wolosz-portrait-undated.jpg"],["FamilySearch memory","https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/122056242"],["Hilda's obituary","https://www.legacy.com/us/obituaries/citizensvoice/name/hilda-wolosz-obituary?id=7822560"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("franconia-racing",1979,"1979","Drag racing reported near the Franconia families","context","History","context","A contemporary report helps explain a family memory of danger.","A Washington Post report described illegal races and arrests in nearby Fullerton Industrial Park. Fairfax County was also growing rapidly during John and Alice's school years.","The report documents a nearby scene, not that John or Alice attended a race or witnessed an incident.","Springfield / Franconia, Virginia",["John Weatherford Sr.","Alice Smith Weatherford"],[["Contemporary report","https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/local/1979/11/18/a-jump-on-dragsters/0343da14-ade3-491c-afc9-af454c2e9c62/"]],"context/franconia-growing-up.md"),
  item("john-alice-marriage",1986,"1986","John Weatherford and Alice Smith marry","smith","Marriage","recorded","One record joins the two Fairfax families.","Their Fairfax marriage return names Alice's parents James Allen Smith and Gladys Louise Blevins, and gives the couple's Miller Drive residences shortly before marriage.","Earlier Smith and Blevins generations still require separate records; the marriage does not establish an immigrant date.","Fairfax County, Virginia",["John Gordon Weatherford Sr.","Alice Marie Smith Weatherford"],[["Original marriage return index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QK9V-J612?lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford-smith.md"),
  item("colette-built",1989,"1989","The Colette Drive house is constructed","smith","Home","recorded","The later Weatherford home takes shape.","Fairfax County's parcel history dates construction to 1989. John and Alice's recorded purchase came later, in 1993.","Construction is not evidence that the family lived there in 1989.","Franconia, Fairfax County, Virginia",["John Weatherford Sr.","Alice Smith Weatherford"],[["Fairfax parcel lookup","https://icare.fairfaxcounty.gov/ffxcare/search/commonsearch.aspx?mode=address"]],"branches/weatherford-smith.md"),
  item("colette-purchase",1993,"1993","John and Alice buy the Colette Drive home","smith","Home","recorded","A $255,000 verified sale appears in county records.","The parcel history records the 1993 purchase. It is a property transaction, not an income or net-worth statement; mortgage and later equity are unknown.","Do not infer inheritance or family wealth from purchase price alone.","Franconia, Fairfax County, Virginia",["John Weatherford Sr.","Alice Smith Weatherford"],[["Fairfax parcel lookup","https://icare.fairfaxcounty.gov/ffxcare/search/commonsearch.aspx?mode=address"]],"branches/weatherford-smith.md"),
  item("garnett-death",2007,"27 Jun 2007","Garnett Weatherford Sr. dies","weatherford","Death","recorded","His obituary remembers Navy and transit service.","The obituary reports Pacific service in the Navy and later work for AB&W and Metro bus systems. His personnel records would tell a much more precise wartime story.","The obituary does not establish a ship, battle, rank or full military timeline.","Northern Virginia",["Garnett Burnell Weatherford Sr."],[["Garnett's obituary","https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/local/2007/07/03/obituaries/43ba782a-181c-4c59-a78e-7d361d86bf4a/"]],"branches/weatherford.md"),
  item("gladys-death",2008,"2008","Gladys Blevins Smith’s memorial names five children","smith","Death","recorded","A published family notice confirms Alice’s sibling group.","The memorial names Jane, James Howard, Timothy, Alice and Carolyn as Gladys's children. Original marriage returns independently connect Jane, James Howard and Alice to the same parents.","Birth order and some individual dates remain open.","Northern Virginia",["Gladys Louise Blevins Smith","Alice Smith Weatherford"],[["Gladys memorial","https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/45796517/gladys_louise-smith"]],"branches/smith.md"),
  item("fred-death",2023,"29 Jul 2023","Fred Kramer’s obituary ties three generations","kramer","Death","recorded","It identifies his parents, brother and service story.","The notice names Ferdinand Louis and Mary Andrews Kramer as Fred's parents. It reports Army Reserve service and Techneglas work, connecting household records to a remembered life.","An obituary is a family report; verify disputed early vital details against original certificates.","Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania",["Fred / Ferdinand Francis Kramer (1939)"],[["Fred's obituary","https://www.legacy.com/person/ferdinand-francis-kramer-%28fred%29-52588560"]],"branches/kramer.md"),
  item("monty-birth",2025,"17 Apr 2025","Monty the Pembroke Welsh Corgi is born","monty","Birth","recorded","His AKC certificate opens a different family tree.","The photographed registration certificate names him Triple H Knight Shadow From Cedar Springs, with sire GCHB CH Triple H Bechanci Knight to Remember and dam Triple H Shadow Dancer RN.","The certificate gives birth and registered parentage, but not his arrival date in Justin's home or older ancestors beyond those checked separately.","Birthplace not stated",["Monty","Triple H Bechanci Knight to Remember","Triple H Shadow Dancer RN"],[["Family AKC certificate",ROOT+"sources/pets/monty-akc-certificate.jpg"]],"branches/monty.md"),
  item("kramers-muncy-now",2026,"Now, family-supplied","Melissa and Paul Kramer live in Muncy Township","miller","Home","family","Their home brings the family story back near Mabel's reported birthplace.","Justin says his parents Melissa Miller Kramer and Paul Kramer live in the Muncy postal area. The Census geocoder places the address in Muncy Township, Lycoming County. Melissa is the daughter of Paul Miller, who is Mabel/Pearl's son. This is a regional return across generations from Muncy Valley through Berwick to today's Muncy area.","Muncy Valley and Muncy Township are different places; Mabel's exact 1919 locality and the later move dates need records. The map marks a township area, not a living person's house. Paul Miller and Paul Kramer are different people.","Muncy Township, Lycoming County, Pennsylvania",["Paul Miller","Melissa Miller Kramer","Paul Kramer","Mabel/Pearl Reese Wilson Miller"],[["Family-supplied place and map",ROOT+"context/family-place-sequences.md"],["Muncy Valley locality","https://www.sullivancountypa.gov/municipalities/shrewsbury-township"]],"context/family-place-sequences.md"),
  item("danville-knitting-1911",1911,"June 1911","A photographer records Danville textile workers","weatherford","History","context","A period image shows the city's industrial setting.","Lewis Hine photographed workers outside the Danville Knitting Works office, reporting that he had seen children at work. George Ira Weatherford's later draft card named Danville Knitting Mills as his 1940 employer.","The two business names have not been proved to denote the same factory. The photo predates George's documented job by 29 years and identifies no Weatherford.","Danville, Virginia",["George Ira Weatherford, later city resident"],[["Library of Congress photograph","https://www.loc.gov/item/2018676502/"]],"branches/weatherford.md#garnetts-brother-george-a-second-transit-and-military-story"),
  item("george-ira-draft-1940",1940,"16 Oct 1940","George Ira Weatherford names his mother and mill employer","weatherford","Work","recorded","A signed card links him to Duffy's family and Danville Knitting Mills.","George Ira's draft registration gives a 15 September 1918 birth, his mother Mrs. Doctor Duffy Weatherford as contact, and Danville Knitting Mills as employer. An independent Social Security index names Duffy and Annie Neathery as parents.","Registration alone does not show active service; a separate enlistment index dates Army entry to 8 August 1944.","Danville, Virginia",["George Ira Weatherford","Doctor Duffy Weatherford","Annie Neathery Weatherford"],[["Saved original card",ROOT+"sources/records/george-ira-weatherford-draft-card-1940.jpg"],["Army enlistment index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:K8TF-L5V?lang=en"]],"branches/weatherford-smith-generations.md"),
  item("george-betty-1951",1951,"5 Jan 1951","George and Betty's divorce record reveals an AB&W job","weatherford","Work","recorded","The original abstract confirms his transit employer and an earlier marriage.","George Ira and Betty Jane Davis Weatherford married in October 1935; their 1940 household includes son Laurence P. The divorce abstract records a January 1951 decree, one minor child, and AB&W Transit Co. as George's employer. Mildred Wilt's family history reports his later June 1951 marriage to Mildred.","The marriage index differs from the divorce abstract by one day. George's recorded age 37 conflicts with the 1918 birth repeated on his signed card and Social Security index. The later Mildred marriage date and his reported Bronze Star need original records.","Arlington, Virginia",["George Ira Weatherford","Betty Jane Davis Weatherford","Laurence P. Weatherford","Mildred Oletta Wilt, later wife in family history"],[["Saved divorce abstract",ROOT+"sources/records/george-ira-betty-davis-divorce-1951.jpg"],["1940 census index","https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VR15-N6W?lang=en"],["Mildred's family history","https://www.waltwarnickwesternmarylandfamilyhistory.com/lee-family-book-1"]],"branches/weatherford-smith-generations.md")
];

